PathwayData.put("data/reading_writing/units/L07U4.json",{"strand":"reading_writing","level":7,"cefr":"B1+","unit":4,"theme":"Natural Disasters","essential_question":"How can communities prepare for and recover from disasters?","schema_version":"1.0","core_words":["earthquake","volcano","hurricane","tsunami","wildfire","evacuate","emergency","warning","damage","shelter","survivor","forecast","aftermath","relief"],"recycled_words":["weather","storm","flood","safety","root","heading","main idea","sequence","because","as a result","should","source","summary"],"rubrics":[{"id":"RW-RUBRIC-5T","title":"Analytic writing rubric","traits":[{"name":"Ideas","name_ar":"الأفكار","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"My ideas are clear, interesting and fully developed with strong details.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري واضحة وممتعة ومدعومة بتفاصيل قوية."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"My ideas are clear and have enough details.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري واضحة ومعها تفاصيل كافية."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"My main idea is there but needs more details.","descriptor_ar":"فكرتي الرئيسية موجودة لكنها تحتاج إلى تفاصيل أكثر."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"My ideas are unclear or very few.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري غير واضحة أو قليلة جدًا."}]},{"name":"Organization","name_ar":"التنظيم","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"Clear beginning, middle and end; ideas flow with linking words.","descriptor_ar":"بداية ووسط ونهاية واضحة، والأفكار مترابطة بكلمات الربط."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"Clear order with some linking words.","descriptor_ar":"ترتيب واضح مع بعض كلمات الربط."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Some order, but parts are mixed up.","descriptor_ar":"يوجد بعض الترتيب لكن بعض الأجزاء غير مرتبة."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Ideas are not in order.","descriptor_ar":"الأفكار غير مرتبة."}]},{"name":"Vocabulary","name_ar":"المفردات","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"I use exact, varied words, including new unit words, correctly.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات دقيقة ومتنوعة، منها كلمات الوحدة الجديدة، بشكل صحيح."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"I use some new words correctly.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم بعض الكلمات الجديدة بشكل صحيح."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"I use simple words; some are repeated or wrong.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات بسيطة، وبعضها مكرر أو غير صحيح."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"I use very few words; the meaning is hard to understand.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات قليلة جدًا ويصعب فهم المعنى."}]},{"name":"Sentence fluency","name_ar":"سلاسة الجمل","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"My sentences are complete, varied in length and easy to read aloud.","descriptor_ar":"جملي كاملة ومتنوعة الطول وسهلة القراءة بصوت عالٍ."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"Most sentences are complete, with some variety.","descriptor_ar":"معظم جملي كاملة مع بعض التنويع."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Many sentences are short or start the same way.","descriptor_ar":"كثير من جملي قصيرة أو تبدأ بالطريقة نفسها."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Sentences are incomplete or run together.","descriptor_ar":"جملي ناقصة أو متداخلة."}]},{"name":"Conventions","name_ar":"قواعد الكتابة","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"Capitals, punctuation, spelling and grammar are almost always correct.","descriptor_ar":"الحروف الكبيرة وعلامات الترقيم والإملاء والقواعد صحيحة دائمًا تقريبًا."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"A few mistakes that do not affect meaning.","descriptor_ar":"أخطاء قليلة لا تؤثر في المعنى."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Several mistakes; the meaning is sometimes unclear.","descriptor_ar":"عدة أخطاء، والمعنى أحيانًا غير واضح."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Many mistakes make it hard to read.","descriptor_ar":"أخطاء كثيرة تجعل القراءة صعبة."}]}]}],"lessons":[{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS1","title":"Word Power: Natural Disasters","lesson_type":"vocabulary","objectives":["read, say and use 14 words about natural disasters and 5 Tier 2 words (impact, vulnerable, predict, coordinate, restore)","use the technical word parts *seismo-* (earthquake), *hydro-* (water), *geo-* (earth), *-logy* (study of), *-ologist* (expert), *-graph* (write/record) and *-meter* (measure)","build and explain science words by combining word parts and checking them in context"],"objectives_ar":["قراءة ونطق واستخدام 14 كلمة عن الكوارث الطبيعية و5 كلمات أكاديمية.","استخدام أجزاء الكلمات العلمية لفهم المصطلحات.","بناء الكلمات العلمية وشرحها بدمج أجزائها والتحقق منها في السياق."],"success_criteria":["I can use the disaster words correctly in sentences.","I can break a science word into parts and explain each part.","I can tell the difference between *-logy*, *-ologist*, *-graph* and *-meter* words."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع استخدام كلمات الكوارث في جمل بشكل صحيح.","أستطيع تقسيم الكلمة العلمية إلى أجزائها وشرح كل جزء.","أستطيع التمييز بين الكلمات المنتهية بـ -logy و-ologist و-graph و-meter."],"map_area":"Vocabulary: Acquisition and Use","vocabulary":[{"word":"earthquake","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɝθˌkweɪk/","definition":"a sudden shaking of the ground caused by movement inside the Earth","arabic":"زلزال","example":"The earthquake shook the windows.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"volcano","pos":"noun","ipa":"/vɑlˈkeɪnoʊ/","definition":"a mountain with an opening through which hot melted rock, ash and gas can come out","arabic":"بركان","example":"Harrat Lunayyir is an old volcanic field in Saudi Arabia.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"hurricane","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈhɝəˌkeɪn/","definition":"a very strong storm with extremely fast, spinning winds","arabic":"إعصار","example":"The hurricane brought heavy rain to the coast.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"tsunami","pos":"noun","ipa":"/tsuˈnɑmi/","definition":"a series of huge sea waves, usually caused by an earthquake under the ocean","arabic":"تسونامي / أمواج مدّية زلزالية","example":"A tsunami warning was sent to the coast.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"wildfire","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈwaɪldˌfaɪər/","definition":"a large fire that spreads quickly through forests or grassland","arabic":"حريق غابات / حريق بري","example":"Strong winds spread the wildfire.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"evacuate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɪˈvækjuˌeɪt/","definition":"to move people from a dangerous place to a safe place","arabic":"يُخلي","example":"The town was evacuated before the storm.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"emergency","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ɪˈmɝdʒənsi/","definition":"a sudden, dangerous situation that needs immediate action","arabic":"حالة طوارئ","example":"In an emergency, stay calm and follow instructions.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V07","tier":"core"},{"word":"warning","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈwɔrnɪŋ/","definition":"a message that tells people about possible danger","arabic":"تحذير / إنذار","example":"The phone showed a flood warning.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V08","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V08","tier":"core"},{"word":"damage","pos":"noun / verb","ipa":"/ˈdæmɪdʒ/","definition":"physical harm to something; to harm something","arabic":"ضرر / يُتلف","example":"The storm caused damage to many roofs.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V09","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V09","tier":"core"},{"word":"shelter","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈʃɛltər/","definition":"a place that protects people from danger or bad weather","arabic":"مأوى / ملجأ","example":"Families stayed in a shelter for three nights.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V10","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V10","tier":"core"},{"word":"survivor","pos":"noun","ipa":"/sərˈvaɪvər/","definition":"a person who stays alive after a dangerous event","arabic":"ناجٍ","example":"Rescue teams helped the survivors.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V11","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V11","tier":"core"},{"word":"forecast","pos":"noun / verb","ipa":"/ˈfɔrˌkæst/","definition":"a statement about what will probably happen, especially with the weather; to say this","arabic":"توقّع / تنبؤ (بالطقس)","example":"The forecast warned of heavy rain.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V12","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V12","tier":"core"},{"word":"aftermath","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈæftərˌmæθ/","definition":"the period after a disaster, when people deal with its effects","arabic":"العواقب / ما بعد الكارثة","example":"In the aftermath, volunteers cleaned the streets.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V13","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V13","tier":"core"},{"word":"relief","pos":"noun","ipa":"/rɪˈlif/","definition":"help such as food, water and medicine given to people in need after a disaster","arabic":"إغاثة","example":"Relief trucks arrived with water and blankets.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V14","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V14","tier":"core"},{"word":"impact","pos":"noun / verb","ipa":"/ˈɪmˌpækt/","definition":"a strong effect; to have a strong effect on something","arabic":"أثر / يؤثّر","example":"The flood had a huge impact on farms.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V15","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V15","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"vulnerable","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈvʌlnərəbəl/","definition":"easily hurt or harmed","arabic":"مُعرَّض للخطر / هشّ","example":"Old buildings are vulnerable to earthquakes.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V16","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V16","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"predict","pos":"verb","ipa":"/prɪˈdɪkt/","definition":"to say what will happen in the future","arabic":"يتنبّأ","example":"Scientists cannot predict exactly when an earthquake will happen.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V17","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V17","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"coordinate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/koʊˈɔrdəˌneɪt/","definition":"to organize people or groups so that they work well together","arabic":"ينسّق","example":"The civil defense coordinated the rescue teams.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V18","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V18","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"restore","pos":"verb","ipa":"/rɪˈstɔr/","definition":"to bring something back to how it was before","arabic":"يُعيد / يستعيد","example":"Workers restored electricity after the storm.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V19","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V19","tier":"tier2"}],"explanation":{"text":"Science texts are full of long **technical words**, but many of them are built from a few **Greek word parts**. Learn the parts and you can unlock the words: ***seismo-*** = **earthquake / shaking**, ***hydro-*** = **water**, ***geo-*** = **earth**.\n\nEndings tell you **what kind** of word it is: ***-logy*** = **the study of** (a science), ***-ologist*** = **an expert who studies it**, ***-graph*** = **something that writes or records**, ***-meter*** = **something that measures**. So *seismology* is the study of earthquakes, and a *seismologist* is a scientist who studies them.\n\nUse the **3-step strategy**: (1) **Split** the word into parts. (2) **Translate** each part. (3) **Check** the meaning in the sentence. Notice how the ending changes the job of the word: *geology* (a subject), *geologist* (a person), *geological* (an adjective).","examples":["***seismology*** = seismo (earthquake) + logy (study of) → the study of earthquakes.","***seismologist*** = seismo + ologist → a scientist who studies earthquakes.","***seismograph*** = seismo + graph (record) → an instrument that records the shaking of the ground.","***hydrology*** = hydro (water) + logy → the study of water on and under the Earth.","***hydroelectric*** = hydro + electric → electricity made from moving water.","***dehydrated*** = de (remove) + hydr (water) + ated → having lost too much water from the body.","***geology*** = geo (earth) + logy → the study of rocks and the Earth.","***thermometer*** = thermo (heat) + meter (measure) → an instrument that measures temperature.","In context: ***Seismologists at the Saudi Geological Survey recorded the earthquakes with seismometers.***"],"common_errors":["✗ A seismology studies earthquakes. → ✓ A seismologist studies earthquakes. (-logy = subject; -ologist = person.)","✗ The seismograph measures the temperature. → ✓ A thermometer measures temperature; a seismograph records shaking. (Know the root.)","✗ Hydrology is the study of hydrogen gas. → ✓ Hydrology is the study of water. (hydro = water.)","✗ Evacuate the danger from the people. → ✓ Evacuate the people from the danger zone. (evacuate + people/place.)","✗ The storm made many damages. → ✓ The storm caused a lot of damage. (*damage* (harm) is uncountable.)","✗ The weather forecast predict rain. → ✓ The weather forecast predicts rain. (Subject–verb agreement.)","✗ tsunami pronounced /tə-soo-nah-mee/ → ✓ /tsuˈnɑmi/ – one syllable *tsu* (Pronunciation.)"],"arabic":"تمتلئ النصوص العلمية بكلمات تقنية طويلة، لكن كثيرًا منها مبني من أجزاء يونانية قليلة: seismo- بمعنى زلزال أو اهتزاز، وhydro- بمعنى ماء، وgeo- بمعنى أرض.\n\nتدل النهايات على نوع الكلمة: -logy علم أو دراسة، و-ologist العالِم المختص، و-graph أداة تسجّل، و-meter أداة تقيس. فـ seismology علم الزلازل، وseismologist عالم الزلازل.\n\nاستخدم استراتيجية الخطوات الثلاث: قسّم الكلمة إلى أجزاء، وترجم كل جزء، ثم تحقّق من المعنى في الجملة. ولاحظ كيف تغيّر النهاية وظيفة الكلمة: geology وgeologist وgeological.\n\nانتبه: damage بمعنى الضرر اسم غير معدود، فلا نقول damages."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"The ground shakes, the sea rises, the wind roars. How can communities prepare for and recover from disasters?","on_screen":"Natural Disasters 🌍","visual":"Calm illustrations of the Earth with icons for earthquakes, storms, floods and fires."},{"time":"00:30–01:30","narration":"Earthquake, volcano, hurricane, tsunami, wildfire, evacuate, emergency, warning, damage, shelter, survivor, forecast, aftermath, relief!","on_screen":"earthquake · volcano · hurricane · tsunami · wildfire · evacuate · emergency · warning · damage · shelter · survivor · forecast · aftermath · relief","visual":"Each word appears on a safety poster."},{"time":"01:30–02:10","narration":"Scientists try to predict risks, leaders coordinate help, and communities restore what was lost. Some places are more vulnerable – the impact can be huge.","on_screen":"impact · vulnerable · predict · coordinate · restore","visual":"A control room with maps; a team rebuilding a school."},{"time":"02:10–03:30","narration":"Seismo means earthquake. Hydro means water. Geo means earth. Add -logy, -ologist, -graph or -meter – and unlock the science!","on_screen":"seismo- · hydro- · geo- · -logy · -ologist · -graph · -meter","visual":"Word parts click together like puzzle pieces."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Split it, translate the parts, check the sentence!","on_screen":"3 steps to unlock science words","visual":"A long word splits into colored parts."}],"captions_ar":["تهتز الأرض، ويرتفع البحر، وتعصف الرياح. كيف تستعد المجتمعات للكوارث وتتعافى منها؟","زلزال، بركان، إعصار، تسونامي، حريق بري، يُخلي، طوارئ، إنذار، ضرر، مأوى، ناجٍ، توقّع، ما بعد الكارثة، إغاثة!","يحاول العلماء التنبؤ بالمخاطر، وينسّق القادة المساعدة، وتستعيد المجتمعات ما فقدته. بعض الأماكن أكثر عرضة للخطر، وقد يكون الأثر كبيرًا.","seismo تعني زلزال، وhydro تعني ماء، وgeo تعني أرض. أضف -logy أو -ologist أو -graph أو -meter وافتح أبواب العلم!","قسّم الكلمة، وترجم أجزاءها، وتحقّق من الجملة!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, calm, reassuring male voice, speed 1.0."},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct disaster word.","instructions_ar":"اختر كلمة الكوارث الصحيحة.","items":[{"q":"The ___ on the news said there would be heavy rain and strong winds tomorrow.","answer":"forecast","explanation":"Forecast.","explanation_ar":"النشرة / التوقعات.","options":["survivor","forecast","aftermath","shelter"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Because the river was rising quickly, the police ___ the families living near it.","answer":"evacuated","explanation":"Evacuate.","explanation_ar":"أخلت.","options":["restored","evacuated","predicted","damaged"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"A huge ___ crossed the ocean after the undersea earthquake.","answer":"tsunami","explanation":"Tsunami.","explanation_ar":"تسونامي.","options":["wildfire","volcano","tsunami","forecast"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"After the storm, the families stayed in a school that was used as a ___.","answer":"shelter","explanation":"Shelter.","explanation_ar":"مأوى.","options":["shelter","warning","hurricane","survivor"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Trucks carrying water, blankets and medicine brought ___ to the flooded town.","answer":"relief","explanation":"Relief.","explanation_ar":"إغاثة.","options":["damage","emergency","aftermath","relief"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"In the ___ of the flood, volunteers spent weeks cleaning homes and schools.","answer":"aftermath","explanation":"Aftermath.","explanation_ar":"ما بعد الكارثة.","options":["forecast","warning","volcano","aftermath"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS1-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Use the word parts. Choose.","instructions_ar":"استخدم أجزاء الكلمات: اختر.","items":[{"q":"*Seismo-* means *earthquake* and *-ologist* means *an expert who studies*. Who is a *seismologist*?","answer":"a scientist who studies earthquakes","explanation":"Word parts.","explanation_ar":"عالم زلازل.","options":["a doctor who treats bones","a person who builds houses","a pilot who flies in storms","a scientist who studies earthquakes"],"dok":1,"skill":"word parts","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What does a *seismograph* do?","answer":"It records the shaking of the ground.","explanation":"seismo + graph.","explanation_ar":"يسجّل الاهتزاز.","options":["It measures the temperature of the air.","It records the shaking of the ground.","It studies water under the ground.","It makes electricity from rivers."],"dok":2,"skill":"word parts","stage":"guided"},{"q":"*Hydro-* means *water*. What is *hydroelectric* power?","answer":"electricity made from moving water","explanation":"hydro + electric.","explanation_ar":"كهرباء من الماء.","options":["electricity made from the sun","electricity from earthquakes","electricity made from moving water","electricity stored in batteries"],"dok":2,"skill":"word parts","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which word means *the study of rocks and the Earth*?","answer":"geology","explanation":"-logy = study of.","explanation_ar":"علم الأرض.","options":["geology","geologist","geological","geograph"],"dok":2,"skill":"word parts","stage":"guided"},{"q":"A hiker who has not drunk enough water in the desert is ___.","answer":"dehydrated","explanation":"de + hydr.","explanation_ar":"مصاب بالجفاف.","options":["hydroelectric","seismic","dehydrated","geological"],"dok":2,"skill":"word parts","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence uses *-meter* correctly?","answer":"A thermometer measures how hot or cold something is.","explanation":"-meter = measure.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["A thermometer measures how hot or cold something is.","A thermometer records earthquakes.","A thermometer is a person who studies heat.","A thermometer is the study of heat."],"dok":3,"skill":"word parts","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS1-A3","type":"gap_fill","stage":"freer","instructions":"Freer practice: Use the word bank. Each word fits one sentence.","instructions_ar":"استخدم بنك الكلمات.","items":[{"q":"Scientists cannot ________ exactly when the next earthquake will happen.","answer":"predict","explanation":"Say in advance.","explanation_ar":"يتنبؤوا.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Houses built on the riverbank are especially ________ to floods.","answer":"vulnerable","explanation":"Easily harmed.","explanation_ar":"معرّضة.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"The civil defense ________ the work of firefighters, doctors and volunteers.","answer":"coordinated","explanation":"Organized together.","explanation_ar":"نسّق.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"It took three days to ________ electricity to the whole town.","answer":"restore","explanation":"Bring back.","explanation_ar":"تُعاد.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"The drought had a serious ________ on farmers and their animals.","answer":"impact","explanation":"Strong effect.","explanation_ar":"أثر.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"The strong winds caused serious ________ to roofs and trees.","answer":"damage","explanation":"Harm.","explanation_ar":"أضرارًا.","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS1-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write with the new words and word parts.","instructions_ar":"اكتب باستخدام الكلمات وأجزائها.","items":[{"q":"Explain the difference between *geology* and *geologist* in two sentences.","answer":"Geology is the study of rocks and the Earth. A geologist is a scientist who studies them.","explanation":"-logy vs. -ologist.","explanation_ar":"العلم والعالِم.","dok":2,"skill":"word parts","stage":"production"},{"q":"Break *hydrology* into parts and use it in a sentence about Saudi Arabia.","answer":"hydro (water) + logy (study of). Hydrology helps scientists find water under the deserts of Saudi Arabia.","explanation":"Word parts.","explanation_ar":"أجزاء الكلمة.","dok":2,"skill":"word parts","stage":"production"},{"q":"Describe what people should do when they receive a flood warning, using *evacuate* and *shelter*.","answer":"When people receive a flood warning, they should follow official instructions, evacuate low areas quickly and go to the nearest safe shelter.","explanation":"Use the words.","explanation_ar":"استخدم الكلمات.","dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"production"},{"q":"Why are some communities more vulnerable to disasters than others? Give one example.","answer":"Some communities are more vulnerable because their buildings are old or they live near rivers or coasts. For example, a village with mud-brick houses may suffer more damage in an earthquake.","explanation":"Explain.","explanation_ar":"اشرح.","dok":3,"skill":"connect","stage":"production"}]}],"game":{"name":"Science Word Lab","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Virtual lab: the child pours a root beaker into an ending test tube; correct words bubble and show a picture and a sentence; impossible words fizz out. Each new word fills the lab notebook.","Teams take turns choosing one beaker and one test tube.","If they make a real word, they explain its meaning and use it in a sentence about disasters or science.","If the word is not real, the other team may steal the turn by making a real word.","Teams record each word in their lab report.","Real word = 1 point; correct meaning = 1 point; good sentence = 1 point."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Which word means *to move people from a dangerous place to a safe place*?","answer":"evacuate","explanation":"Evacuate.","explanation_ar":"يُخلي.","options":["forecast","restore","evacuate","predict"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"What does *hydro-* mean?","answer":"water","explanation":"Word part.","explanation_ar":"ماء.","options":["water","earth","earthquake","heat"],"dok":1,"skill":"word parts"},{"q":"*Old buildings are **vulnerable** to earthquakes.* *Vulnerable* means ___.","answer":"easily harmed","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"معرّضة للخطر.","options":["very strong","brand new","very tall","easily harmed"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"What does the ending *-ologist* tell you about a word?","answer":"It names a person who studies a subject.","explanation":"Word part.","explanation_ar":"شخص مختص.","options":["It names an instrument that measures.","It names a person who studies a subject.","It names a kind of storm.","It makes the word negative."],"dok":1,"skill":"word parts"},{"q":"Which sentence about the storm is correct?","answer":"The storm caused a lot of damage to the coast.","explanation":"damage = uncountable.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The storm caused a lot of damage to the coast.","The storm caused many damages to the coast.","The storm causes a lot of damages to the coast yesterday.","The storm caused a lot of damage at the coast to."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which word means *an instrument that records the shaking of the ground*?","answer":"seismograph","explanation":"Word parts.","explanation_ar":"مِرسمة الزلازل.","options":["seismology","thermometer","hydrology","seismograph"],"dok":2,"skill":"word parts"},{"q":"Which is the BEST meaning of *aftermath* in *the aftermath of the flood*?","answer":"the period after the flood when people deal with its effects","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"ما بعد الفيضان.","options":["the math lesson after the flood","the period after the flood when people deal with its effects","the warning before the flood","the cause of the flood"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"*Geo-* means *earth*. Which word means *related to the study of the Earth*?","answer":"geological","explanation":"Word part + suffix.","explanation_ar":"جيولوجي (صفة).","options":["geologist","geological","geology class","geograph"],"dok":2,"skill":"word parts"},{"q":"A student reads *The hydrologist checked the wadi after the rain*. What is the BEST way to work out *hydrologist*?","answer":"Split it: hydro (water) + ologist (expert) → a water scientist.","explanation":"Strategy.","explanation_ar":"قسّم الكلمة.","options":["Guess from the first letter.","Skip the word.","Look only at the word *rain*.","Split it: hydro (water) + ologist (expert) → a water scientist."],"dok":3,"skill":"word parts"},{"q":"Why is it useful to learn technical word parts before reading a science article?","answer":"One part can help you understand many difficult words in the article.","explanation":"Strategy.","explanation_ar":"جزء واحد يفتح كلمات كثيرة.","options":["They tell you the author's opinion.","They make the article shorter.","One part can help you understand many difficult words in the article.","They replace the need to read."],"dok":3,"skill":"word parts"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'earthquake': a cracked road with shaking lines"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'volcano': a cone-shaped mountain with smoke rising"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'hurricane': a spiral storm seen from space"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'tsunami': a giant wave approaching a coastline"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'wildfire': flames moving across dry grassland"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'evacuate': families boarding buses with bags"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'emergency': an ambulance with flashing lights"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V08","prompt":"Picture for the word 'warning': a phone screen with a red alert"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V09","prompt":"Picture for the word 'damage': a house with a broken roof"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V10","prompt":"Picture for the word 'shelter': rows of tents in a safe camp"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V11","prompt":"Picture for the word 'survivor': a rescue worker helping a man walk"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V12","prompt":"Picture for the word 'forecast': a weather presenter beside a map"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V13","prompt":"Picture for the word 'aftermath': volunteers clearing mud from a street"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V14","prompt":"Picture for the word 'relief': a truck unloading boxes of supplies"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V15","prompt":"Picture for the word 'impact': a stone hitting water with ripples"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V16","prompt":"Picture for the word 'vulnerable': a cracked old wall beside a strong new one"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V17","prompt":"Picture for the word 'predict': a scientist looking at a graph with a question mark"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V18","prompt":"Picture for the word 'coordinate': a leader directing teams with a radio"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V19","prompt":"Picture for the word 'restore': a worker fixing power lines"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-01","prompt":"Natural disasters and the people who prepare and help."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-02","prompt":"Icons for disaster words."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS1-03","prompt":"Building science words from parts."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V01","script":"earthquake. The earthquake shook the windows."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V02","script":"volcano. Harrat Lunayyir is an old volcanic field in Saudi Arabia."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V03","script":"hurricane. The hurricane brought heavy rain to the coast."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V04","script":"tsunami. A tsunami warning was sent to the coast."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V05","script":"wildfire. Strong winds spread the wildfire."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V06","script":"evacuate. The town was evacuated before the storm."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V07","script":"emergency. In an emergency, stay calm and follow instructions."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V08","script":"warning. The phone showed a flood warning."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V09","script":"damage. The storm caused damage to many roofs."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V10","script":"shelter. Families stayed in a shelter for three nights."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V11","script":"survivor. Rescue teams helped the survivors."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V12","script":"forecast. The forecast warned of heavy rain."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V13","script":"aftermath. In the aftermath, volunteers cleaned the streets."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V14","script":"relief. Relief trucks arrived with water and blankets."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V15","script":"impact. The flood had a huge impact on farms."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V16","script":"vulnerable. Old buildings are vulnerable to earthquakes."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V17","script":"predict. Scientists cannot predict exactly when an earthquake will happen."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V18","script":"coordinate. The civil defense coordinated the rescue teams."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-V19","script":"restore. Workers restored electricity after the storm."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS1-VID","script":"The ground shakes, the sea rises, the wind roars. How can communities prepare for and recover from disasters? Earthquake, volcano, hurricane, tsunami, wildfire, evacuate, emergency, warning, damage, shelter, survivor, forecast, aftermath, relief! Scientists try to predict risks, leaders coordinate help, and communities restore what was lost. Some places are more vulnerable – the impact can be huge. Seismo means earthquake. Hydro means water. Geo means earth. Add -logy, -ologist, -graph or -meter – and unlock the science! Split it, translate the parts, check the sentence!"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS2","title":"When the Earth Shakes","lesson_type":"close_reading","objectives":["read a science article of about 560 words about earthquakes","explain how an earthquake happens as a sequence of steps, using technical vocabulary","use headings, a diagram and context clues to find the main idea of each section"],"objectives_ar":["قراءة مقالة علمية من نحو 560 كلمة عن الزلازل.","شرح حدوث الزلزال في خطوات متسلسلة باستخدام المفردات العلمية.","استخدام العناوين والرسم التوضيحي وقرائن السياق لتحديد الفكرة الرئيسة لكل قسم."],"success_criteria":["I can explain the six steps of an earthquake in order.","I can explain *fault*, *focus*, *epicenter* and *magnitude* in my own words.","I can write the main idea of each section in one sentence."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع شرح خطوات حدوث الزلزال الست بالترتيب.","أستطيع شرح المصطلحات العلمية بأسلوبي.","أستطيع كتابة الفكرة الرئيسة لكل قسم في جملة واحدة."],"map_area":"Informational Text: Key Ideas and Details","vocabulary":[{"word":"crust","pos":"noun","ipa":"/krʌst/","definition":"the hard outer layer of the Earth","arabic":"القشرة الأرضية","example":"The Earth's crust is broken into plates.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"tectonic plate","pos":"noun","ipa":"/tɛkˈtɑnɪk ˌpleɪt/","definition":"one of the huge moving pieces of the Earth's crust","arabic":"صفيحة تكتونية","example":"The Arabian Plate is moving away from Africa.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"fault","pos":"noun","ipa":"/fɔlt/","definition":"a long crack in the Earth's crust where rocks can move","arabic":"صدع","example":"The rocks slipped along a fault.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"seismic","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈsaɪzmɪk/","definition":"related to earthquakes or to waves that travel through the Earth","arabic":"زلزالي","example":"Seismic waves spread in every direction.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"epicenter","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɛpɪˌsɛntər/","definition":"the point on the Earth's surface directly above where an earthquake starts","arabic":"مركز الزلزال السطحي","example":"The shaking was strongest at the epicenter.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"magnitude","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈmæɡnəˌtud/","definition":"a number that shows how big an earthquake is","arabic":"قوة (الزلزال) / شدة","example":"The earthquake had a magnitude of 5.4.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"landslide","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈlændˌslaɪd/","definition":"a mass of earth and rocks that suddenly falls down a slope","arabic":"انهيار أرضي","example":"The earthquake triggered a landslide in the mountains.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"Science articles explain **how** and **why** things happen. They use **headings** to divide the topic into sections, **technical vocabulary** (often defined in the text) and **text features** such as diagrams and captions. Before reading closely, preview the headings: they show you the **structure** of the article.\n\nMany science texts explain a **process** – a series of steps in order. Look for **sequence signals**: *where…, when…, then, as a result, finally*. Turn the process into a **flowchart**: *plates move → edges get stuck → pressure builds → rocks break along a fault → energy is released as seismic waves → shaking is strongest at the epicenter*.\n\nWriters often **define** technical words right in the sentence: *a crack called a fault*; *the point on the surface directly above it is the epicenter*; *a tsunami, a series of giant waves*. Look for clue words such as **called**, **is**, **means** and **commas** or **dashes** that introduce a definition. Then write the **main idea** of each section in one sentence.","examples":["Heading → section topic: ***How an Earthquake Happens*** → this section explains the process.","Definition clue (*called*): ***The Earth's outer layer, called the crust…***","Definition clue (*is*): ***The point on the surface directly above it is the epicenter.***","Definition clue (comma): ***a tsunami, a series of giant waves that race toward the coast***","Comparison that helps: ***The plates move … about as fast as your fingernails grow.***","Sequence signal: ***When the pressure becomes too great, the rocks suddenly break.***","Number that explains size: ***A magnitude 6 earthquake releases about 32 times more energy than a magnitude 5.***","Main idea – Measuring the Shaking: ***Seismologists use seismometers and magnitude to measure earthquakes.***","Local example: ***The Saudi Geological Survey runs a national network of seismic stations.***"],"common_errors":["✗ Skipping the headings. → ✓ Preview the headings first to see the structure. (Headings = map.)","✗ Thinking the epicenter is underground. → ✓ The focus is underground; the epicenter is on the surface above it. (Focus vs. epicenter.)","✗ Thinking a magnitude 6 is only a little bigger than a 5. → ✓ It releases about 32 times more energy. (Read the numbers carefully.)","✗ Mixing up the order of the process. → ✓ Use a flowchart with arrows. (Sequence.)","✗ The plates moves very slowly. → ✓ The plates move very slowly. (Plural subject: **move**.)","✗ Copying whole sentences as the main idea. → ✓ Write the main idea in your own short sentence. (Your words.)","✗ Thinking scientists can predict earthquakes exactly. → ✓ They cannot; communities prepare instead. (Read carefully.)"],"arabic":"تشرح المقالات العلمية كيف ولماذا تحدث الأشياء، وتستخدم العناوين لتقسيم الموضوع، والمفردات العلمية التي تُعرّف غالبًا داخل النص، وعناصر مثل الرسوم التوضيحية والتعليقات. استعرض العناوين أولًا لتعرف بناء المقالة.\n\nكثير من النصوص العلمية يشرح عملية في خطوات متتابعة، فابحث عن إشارات التسلسل وحوّل العملية إلى مخطط: تتحرك الصفائح ← تعلق حوافها ← يتراكم الضغط ← تنكسر الصخور على صدع ← تنطلق الطاقة موجات زلزالية ← تكون الهزة أقوى عند المركز السطحي.\n\nيعرّف الكتّاب المصطلحات داخل الجملة بكلمات مثل called وis وmeans أو بالفواصل والشرطات، ثم اكتب الفكرة الرئيسة لكل قسم في جملة واحدة.\n\nانتبه: البؤرة (focus) تحت الأرض، والمركز السطحي (epicenter) فوقها مباشرة."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"May 19, 2009. In Al-Ais, the ground jolts and walls crack. What makes the Earth shake?","on_screen":"When the Earth Shakes 🌍","visual":"A calm map of western Saudi Arabia with a pulsing dot near Al-Ais."},{"time":"00:30–01:40","narration":"The crust is broken into plates that move about as fast as your fingernails grow…","on_screen":"A Planet in Pieces","visual":"An animated globe cracking into plates like an eggshell; the Red Sea slowly widening."},{"time":"01:40–02:40","narration":"Stuck edges, building pressure, a sudden break along a fault – and seismic waves spread out.","on_screen":"How an Earthquake Happens","visual":"A flowchart animation from focus to epicenter."},{"time":"02:40–03:30","narration":"Seismometers, magnitude, early warnings, flexible buildings – and drop, cover, hold on!","on_screen":"Measuring · Effects · Solutions","visual":"A seismometer line, a flexible building swaying safely, students under desks in a drill."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Headings, definitions, sequence signals – read science like a scientist!","on_screen":"Reading a science article closely","visual":"A magnifying glass over headings and definitions."}],"captions_ar":["19 مايو 2009: في العيص تهتز الأرض وتتشقق الجدران. ما الذي يجعل الأرض تهتز؟","القشرة مكسّرة إلى صفائح تتحرك بسرعة نمو أظفارك تقريبًا…","حواف عالقة، وضغط متراكم، وانكسار مفاجئ على صدع، ثم تنتشر الموجات الزلزالية.","أجهزة رصد الزلازل، والقوة، والإنذار المبكر، والمباني المرنة، و«انخفض واحتمِ وتمسّك»!","العناوين والتعريفات وإشارات التسلسل: اقرأ العلوم كما يقرؤها العالِم!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, calm, clear female voice, speed 1.0."},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"When the Earth Shakes","body":"On the evening of May 19, 2009, people in the town of Al-Ais, in western Saudi Arabia, felt the ground jolt beneath their feet. Walls cracked, and dishes rattled on their shelves. It was one of the strongest of more than 30,000 earthquakes that struck the area between April and June that year. Most were too small to feel, but this one measured 5.4. To keep people safe, the authorities evacuated about 40,000 residents until the shaking stopped. What causes the ground to shake like this, and what can people do about it?\n\n**A Planet in Pieces** – The Earth's outer layer, called the crust, is not one solid shell. It is broken into huge pieces called tectonic plates, which fit together like the pieces of a cracked eggshell. The plates move very slowly, usually a few centimeters a year, which is about as fast as your fingernails grow. The Arabian Plate, for example, is slowly moving away from Africa, and this movement is gradually widening the Red Sea.\n\n**How an Earthquake Happens** – Where two plates meet, their edges do not slide smoothly. They get stuck, and pressure builds up in the rocks for years or even centuries. When the pressure becomes too great, the rocks suddenly break and slip along a crack called a fault. The stored energy is released as seismic waves, which travel through the ground in every direction. The point underground where the rocks break is called the focus. The point on the surface directly above it is the epicenter, where the shaking is usually strongest. Earthquakes can also happen when hot melted rock pushes its way up through the crust, and scientists believe this is what happened near Al-Ais.\n\n**Measuring the Shaking** – Seismologists record earthquakes with instruments called seismometers. They describe the size of an earthquake with a number called its magnitude. Each step up the scale means a much bigger earthquake: a magnitude 6 earthquake releases about 32 times more energy than a magnitude 5. In Saudi Arabia, the Saudi Geological Survey runs a national network of seismic stations that monitors earthquakes day and night.\n\n**The Effects of Earthquakes** – Strong earthquakes can damage buildings, roads and bridges. In mountains, they can trigger landslides. When an earthquake happens under the ocean, it can push up the seafloor and create a tsunami, a series of giant waves that race toward the coast. In 2011, an earthquake of about magnitude 9 off the coast of Japan caused a tsunami that flooded large areas of land.\n\n**Problems and Solutions** – Scientists still cannot predict exactly when or where an earthquake will strike, so communities focus on being prepared. Engineers design buildings with strong, flexible frames that bend instead of breaking, and some buildings even stand on special pads that absorb the shaking. Early warning systems, such as the one in Japan, detect the first fast waves and send alerts a few seconds before the strongest shaking arrives. That is just enough time to stop trains and take cover. At school and at home, people practice a simple drill: drop to the ground, take cover under a sturdy table and hold on until the shaking stops.\n\nEarthquakes remind us that the ground beneath our feet is always moving. We cannot stop them, but with knowledge, strong buildings and good planning, communities can be ready when the Earth shakes.","word_count":560,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS2-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L07-U4-LS2-1","genre":"informational","role":"main","difficulty":"800L–950L","glossary":[{"word":"crust","definition":"the hard outer layer of the Earth","arabic":"القشرة الأرضية"},{"word":"seismic waves","definition":"waves of energy that travel through the Earth during an earthquake","arabic":"الموجات الزلزالية"},{"word":"sturdy","definition":"strong and not easily broken","arabic":"متين"}]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the article. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ المقالة واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"When did the 5.4 earthquake strike Al-Ais?","answer":"on May 19, 2009","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"19 مايو 2009.","options":["on June 30, 2011","in April 2023","on May 19, 2009","on May 19, 1990"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"About how many residents were evacuated from the Al-Ais area?","answer":"about 40,000","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"نحو 40 ألفًا.","options":["about 4,000","about 400","about 40,000","about 400,000"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How fast do tectonic plates usually move, according to the article?","answer":"a few centimeters a year","explanation":"A Planet in Pieces.","explanation_ar":"بضعة سنتيمترات سنويًا.","options":["a few centimeters a year","a few meters a day","a kilometer a year","they never move"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which body of water is slowly widening because the Arabian Plate is moving away from Africa?","answer":"the Red Sea","explanation":"A Planet in Pieces.","explanation_ar":"البحر الأحمر.","options":["the Arabian Gulf","the Red Sea","the Mediterranean Sea","the Indian Ocean"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What instrument do seismologists use to record earthquakes?","answer":"seismometers","explanation":"Measuring the Shaking.","explanation_ar":"أجهزة رصد الزلازل.","options":["seismometers","thermometers","telescopes","compasses"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which organization runs Saudi Arabia's national network of seismic stations?","answer":"the Saudi Geological Survey","explanation":"Measuring the Shaking.","explanation_ar":"هيئة المساحة الجيولوجية.","options":["the Ministry of Education","a school science club","the Saudi Space Commission","the Saudi Geological Survey"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS2-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Process, definitions and main ideas. Choose.","instructions_ar":"العملية والتعريفات والأفكار الرئيسة: اختر.","items":[{"q":"What happens just BEFORE the rocks suddenly break and slip along a fault?","answer":"Pressure builds up because the plate edges are stuck.","explanation":"Sequence.","explanation_ar":"يتراكم الضغط.","options":["Seismic waves reach the epicenter.","A tsunami forms.","The plates stop moving forever.","Pressure builds up because the plate edges are stuck."],"dok":2,"skill":"sequence","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which clue word helps you understand *a crack called a fault*?","answer":"called","explanation":"Definition clue.","explanation_ar":"called.","options":["crack","a","called","slip"],"dok":2,"skill":"context clues","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Where is the epicenter of an earthquake?","answer":"on the surface directly above the point where the rocks break","explanation":"Technical vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"على السطح فوق البؤرة.","options":["on the surface directly above the point where the rocks break","deep inside the Earth at the focus","in the ocean only","at the seismometer station"],"dok":2,"skill":"technical vocabulary","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What is the MAIN idea of the section *Problems and Solutions*?","answer":"Because earthquakes cannot be predicted exactly, communities prepare with strong buildings, warnings and drills.","explanation":"Main idea.","explanation_ar":"الفكرة الرئيسة.","options":["Japan has trains.","Tables are sturdy.","Because earthquakes cannot be predicted exactly, communities prepare with strong buildings, warnings and drills.","Earthquakes happen every day."],"dok":2,"skill":"main idea","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Why does the author compare the speed of plates to the growth of fingernails?","answer":"to help readers imagine a very slow speed","explanation":"Author's craft.","explanation_ar":"لتقريب البطء إلى الذهن.","options":["to show that plates are made of nails","to explain how to cut nails","to make the article funny","to help readers imagine a very slow speed"],"dok":3,"skill":"author's craft","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Why does the author begin with the Al-Ais earthquake?","answer":"to connect the science to a real event in Saudi Arabia and hook the reader","explanation":"Introduction.","explanation_ar":"لربط العلم بحدث محلي حقيقي.","options":["to prove that Saudi Arabia has the most earthquakes","to connect the science to a real event in Saudi Arabia and hook the reader","to give instructions for evacuation","to describe a volcano eruption"],"dok":3,"skill":"author's craft","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS2-A3","type":"highlight","stage":"freer","instructions":"Find the evidence: Find the evidence.","instructions_ar":"ابحث عن الدليل.","items":[{"q":"Underline the sentence that explains what the focus of an earthquake is.","answer":"The point underground where the rocks break is called the focus.","explanation":"How an Earthquake Happens.","explanation_ar":"البؤرة.","evidence":"The point underground where the rocks break is called the focus.","text_id":"TXT-RW-L07-U4-LS2-1","dok":1,"skill":"technical vocabulary","stage":"freer","sentences":["On the evening of May 19, 2009, people in the town of Al-Ais, in western Saudi Arabia, felt the ground jolt beneath their feet.","Walls cracked, and dishes rattled on their shelves.","It was one of the strongest of more than 30,000 earthquakes that struck the area between April and June that year.","Most were too small to feel, but this one measured 5.4.","To keep people safe, the authorities evacuated about 40,000 residents until the shaking stopped.","What causes the ground to shake like this, and what can people do about it?","**A Planet in Pieces** – The Earth's outer layer, called the crust, is not one solid shell.","It is broken into huge pieces called tectonic plates, which fit together like the pieces of a cracked eggshell.","The plates move very slowly, usually a few centimeters a year, which is about as fast as your fingernails grow.","The Arabian Plate, for example, is slowly moving away from Africa, and this movement is gradually widening the Red Sea.","**How an Earthquake Happens** – Where two plates meet, their edges do not slide smoothly.","They get stuck, and pressure builds up in the rocks for years or even centuries.","When the pressure becomes too great, the rocks suddenly break and slip along a crack called a fault.","The stored energy is released as seismic waves, which travel through the ground in every direction.","The point underground where the rocks break is called the focus.","The point on the surface directly above it is the epicenter, where the shaking is usually strongest.","Earthquakes can also happen when hot melted rock pushes its way up through the crust, and scientists believe this is what happened near Al-Ais.","**Measuring the Shaking** – Seismologists record earthquakes with instruments called seismometers.","They describe the size of an earthquake with a number called its magnitude.","Each step up the scale means a much bigger earthquake: a magnitude 6 earthquake releases about 32 times more energy than a magnitude 5.","In Saudi Arabia, the Saudi Geological Survey runs a national network of seismic stations that monitors earthquakes day and night.","**The Effects of Earthquakes** – Strong earthquakes can damage buildings, roads and bridges.","In mountains, they can trigger landslides.","When an earthquake happens under the ocean, it can push up the seafloor and create a tsunami, a series of giant waves that race toward the coast.","In 2011, an earthquake of about magnitude 9 off the coast of Japan caused a tsunami that flooded large areas of land.","**Problems and Solutions** – Scientists still cannot predict exactly when or where an earthquake will strike, so communities focus on being prepared.","Engineers design buildings with strong, flexible frames that bend instead of breaking, and some buildings even stand on special pads that absorb the shaking.","Early warning systems, such as the one in Japan, detect the first fast waves and send alerts a few seconds before the strongest shaking arrives.","That is just enough time to stop trains and take cover.","At school and at home, people practice a simple drill: drop to the ground, take cover under a sturdy table and hold on until the shaking stops.","Earthquakes remind us that the ground beneath our feet is always moving.","We cannot stop them, but with knowledge, strong buildings and good planning, communities can be ready when the Earth shakes."]},{"q":"Underline the sentence that explains how an early warning system helps.","answer":"Early warning systems, such as the one in Japan, detect the first fast waves and send alerts a few seconds before the strongest shaking arrives.","explanation":"Problems and Solutions.","explanation_ar":"الإنذار المبكر.","evidence":"Early warning systems, such as the one in Japan, detect the first fast waves and send alerts a few seconds before the strongest shaking arrives.","text_id":"TXT-RW-L07-U4-LS2-1","dok":2,"skill":"problem and solution","stage":"freer","sentences":["On the evening of May 19, 2009, people in the town of Al-Ais, in western Saudi Arabia, felt the ground jolt beneath their feet.","Walls cracked, and dishes rattled on their shelves.","It was one of the strongest of more than 30,000 earthquakes that struck the area between April and June that year.","Most were too small to feel, but this one measured 5.4.","To keep people safe, the authorities evacuated about 40,000 residents until the shaking stopped.","What causes the ground to shake like this, and what can people do about it?","**A Planet in Pieces** – The Earth's outer layer, called the crust, is not one solid shell.","It is broken into huge pieces called tectonic plates, which fit together like the pieces of a cracked eggshell.","The plates move very slowly, usually a few centimeters a year, which is about as fast as your fingernails grow.","The Arabian Plate, for example, is slowly moving away from Africa, and this movement is gradually widening the Red Sea.","**How an Earthquake Happens** – Where two plates meet, their edges do not slide smoothly.","They get stuck, and pressure builds up in the rocks for years or even centuries.","When the pressure becomes too great, the rocks suddenly break and slip along a crack called a fault.","The stored energy is released as seismic waves, which travel through the ground in every direction.","The point underground where the rocks break is called the focus.","The point on the surface directly above it is the epicenter, where the shaking is usually strongest.","Earthquakes can also happen when hot melted rock pushes its way up through the crust, and scientists believe this is what happened near Al-Ais.","**Measuring the Shaking** – Seismologists record earthquakes with instruments called seismometers.","They describe the size of an earthquake with a number called its magnitude.","Each step up the scale means a much bigger earthquake: a magnitude 6 earthquake releases about 32 times more energy than a magnitude 5.","In Saudi Arabia, the Saudi Geological Survey runs a national network of seismic stations that monitors earthquakes day and night.","**The Effects of Earthquakes** – Strong earthquakes can damage buildings, roads and bridges.","In mountains, they can trigger landslides.","When an earthquake happens under the ocean, it can push up the seafloor and create a tsunami, a series of giant waves that race toward the coast.","In 2011, an earthquake of about magnitude 9 off the coast of Japan caused a tsunami that flooded large areas of land.","**Problems and Solutions** – Scientists still cannot predict exactly when or where an earthquake will strike, so communities focus on being prepared.","Engineers design buildings with strong, flexible frames that bend instead of breaking, and some buildings even stand on special pads that absorb the shaking.","Early warning systems, such as the one in Japan, detect the first fast waves and send alerts a few seconds before the strongest shaking arrives.","That is just enough time to stop trains and take cover.","At school and at home, people practice a simple drill: drop to the ground, take cover under a sturdy table and hold on until the shaking stops.","Earthquakes remind us that the ground beneath our feet is always moving.","We cannot stop them, but with knowledge, strong buildings and good planning, communities can be ready when the Earth shakes."]},{"q":"Why do some buildings stand on special pads?","answer":"to absorb the shaking during an earthquake","explanation":"Problems and Solutions.","explanation_ar":"لامتصاص الاهتزاز.","options":["to absorb the shaking during an earthquake","to make them taller","to keep them cool","to stop floods"],"dok":2,"skill":"problem and solution","stage":"freer"},{"q":"What does *jolt* mean in *felt the ground jolt beneath their feet*?","answer":"move suddenly and roughly","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"يهتز فجأة.","options":["become very hot","move suddenly and roughly","turn into water","stay completely still"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary in context","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS2-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Explain the science.","instructions_ar":"اشرح العلم.","items":[{"q":"Explain how an earthquake happens in four to six steps.","answer":"Plates move slowly. Their edges get stuck. Pressure builds up. The rocks break and slip along a fault. Energy travels as seismic waves. The shaking is strongest at the epicenter.","explanation":"Process.","explanation_ar":"العملية.","dok":2,"skill":"sequence","stage":"production"},{"q":"Explain the difference between the focus and the epicenter.","answer":"The focus is the point underground where the rocks break. The epicenter is the point on the surface directly above it.","explanation":"Technical vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"المصطلحات.","dok":2,"skill":"technical vocabulary","stage":"production"},{"q":"Why is a magnitude 6 earthquake much more dangerous than a magnitude 5?","answer":"Because each step up the scale releases much more energy – about 32 times more – so the shaking and damage can be far greater.","explanation":"Numbers.","explanation_ar":"الأرقام.","dok":3,"skill":"inference","stage":"production"},{"q":"Which solution in the article do you think is most important for a school? Explain.","answer":"Practicing the drop, cover and hold on drill, because students can protect themselves immediately, even before help arrives.","explanation":"Evaluate.","explanation_ar":"قيّم.","dok":3,"skill":"problem and solution","stage":"production"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS2-TR-1","type":"timed_reading","instructions":"Timed reading: Read \"When the Earth Shakes\" aloud or silently. Try to finish in the time.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص وحاول أن تنهيه في الوقت المحدد.","items":[{"q":"Read “When the Earth Shakes”.","answer":"560 words","title":"When the Earth Shakes","body":"On the evening of May 19, 2009, people in the town of Al-Ais, in western Saudi Arabia, felt the ground jolt beneath their feet. Walls cracked, and dishes rattled on their shelves. It was one of the strongest of more than 30,000 earthquakes that struck the area between April and June that year. Most were too small to feel, but this one measured 5.4. To keep people safe, the authorities evacuated about 40,000 residents until the shaking stopped. What causes the ground to shake like this, and what can people do about it?\n\n**A Planet in Pieces** – The Earth's outer layer, called the crust, is not one solid shell. It is broken into huge pieces called tectonic plates, which fit together like the pieces of a cracked eggshell. The plates move very slowly, usually a few centimeters a year, which is about as fast as your fingernails grow. The Arabian Plate, for example, is slowly moving away from Africa, and this movement is gradually widening the Red Sea.\n\n**How an Earthquake Happens** – Where two plates meet, their edges do not slide smoothly. They get stuck, and pressure builds up in the rocks for years or even centuries. When the pressure becomes too great, the rocks suddenly break and slip along a crack called a fault. The stored energy is released as seismic waves, which travel through the ground in every direction. The point underground where the rocks break is called the focus. The point on the surface directly above it is the epicenter, where the shaking is usually strongest. Earthquakes can also happen when hot melted rock pushes its way up through the crust, and scientists believe this is what happened near Al-Ais.\n\n**Measuring the Shaking** – Seismologists record earthquakes with instruments called seismometers. They describe the size of an earthquake with a number called its magnitude. Each step up the scale means a much bigger earthquake: a magnitude 6 earthquake releases about 32 times more energy than a magnitude 5. In Saudi Arabia, the Saudi Geological Survey runs a national network of seismic stations that monitors earthquakes day and night.\n\n**The Effects of Earthquakes** – Strong earthquakes can damage buildings, roads and bridges. In mountains, they can trigger landslides. When an earthquake happens under the ocean, it can push up the seafloor and create a tsunami, a series of giant waves that race toward the coast. In 2011, an earthquake of about magnitude 9 off the coast of Japan caused a tsunami that flooded large areas of land.\n\n**Problems and Solutions** – Scientists still cannot predict exactly when or where an earthquake will strike, so communities focus on being prepared. Engineers design buildings with strong, flexible frames that bend instead of breaking, and some buildings even stand on special pads that absorb the shaking. Early warning systems, such as the one in Japan, detect the first fast waves and send alerts a few seconds before the strongest shaking arrives. That is just enough time to stop trains and take cover. At school and at home, people practice a simple drill: drop to the ground, take cover under a sturdy table and hold on until the shaking stops.\n\nEarthquakes remind us that the ground beneath our feet is always moving. We cannot stop them, but with knowledge, strong buildings and good planning, communities can be ready when the Earth shakes.","words":560,"limit":273}],"time_limit_sec":273},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS2-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Earthquake Process Flowchart","instructions":"Organizer: Write the six steps of an earthquake in order, using the technical words from the article.","items":[{"q":"Step 1 – the plates","answer":"Tectonic plates move slowly, a few centimeters a year."},{"q":"Step 2 – the edges","answer":"Where plates meet, their edges get stuck."},{"q":"Step 3 – the pressure","answer":"Pressure builds up in the rocks for years or centuries."},{"q":"Step 4 – the break","answer":"The rocks suddenly break and slip along a fault (at the focus)."},{"q":"Step 5 – the energy","answer":"Energy is released as seismic waves that travel in every direction."},{"q":"Step 6 – the shaking","answer":"The shaking is usually strongest at the epicenter, on the surface above the focus."}]}],"game":{"name":"Quake Sequence Race","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Quake lab: the child drags step cards into the right order along a fault line; when the order is correct, a safe animated quake shows the waves spreading. Then the child matches crust, fault, focus, epicenter and magnitude to their definitions.","Teams receive the six step cards in a mixed order.","They race to stand in a line along the floor fault in the correct order.","Each member reads his or her step aloud.","Then teams match five definition cards to five technical words.","Correct order = 3 points; each correct definition = 1 point."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is the Earth's *crust*?","answer":"the hard outer layer of the Earth","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"القشرة الأرضية.","options":["the hot center of the Earth","a kind of earthquake","the hard outer layer of the Earth","the air around the Earth"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"What is a *fault*?","answer":"a long crack in the Earth's crust where rocks can move","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"صدع.","options":["a mistake in a test","a long crack in the Earth's crust where rocks can move","a type of volcano","a tall wave"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*The earthquake had a **magnitude** of 5.4.* *Magnitude* means ___.","answer":"a number that shows how big an earthquake is","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"القوة.","options":["the place where it happened","a number that shows how big an earthquake is","the time it started","the name of the scientist"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which text feature tells you what each section of a science article is about?","answer":"the headings","explanation":"Text features.","explanation_ar":"العناوين.","options":["the headings","the page numbers","the author's name","the font size"],"dok":1,"skill":"text features"},{"q":"Which sentence about tectonic plates is correct?","answer":"The plates move a few centimeters every year.","explanation":"Agreement.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The plates moves a few centimeters every year.","The plates moving a few centimeters every year.","The plates is moving a few centimeters every year.","The plates move a few centimeters every year."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"What can an earthquake under the ocean cause?","answer":"a tsunami","explanation":"Effects.","explanation_ar":"تسونامي.","options":["a sandstorm","a wildfire","a hurricane","a tsunami"],"dok":2,"skill":"cause and effect"},{"q":"Which phrase in the article signals a DEFINITION?","answer":"the point on the surface directly above it is the epicenter","explanation":"Definition clue.","explanation_ar":"تعريف.","options":["the point on the surface directly above it is the epicenter","On the evening of May 19, 2009","Strong earthquakes can damage buildings","We cannot stop them"],"dok":2,"skill":"context clues"},{"q":"Why do people practice *drop, cover and hold on*?","answer":"to protect themselves from falling objects during the shaking","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"للحماية أثناء الاهتزاز.","options":["to predict when an earthquake will happen","to measure the magnitude","to protect themselves from falling objects during the shaking","to stop the plates from moving"],"dok":2,"skill":"problem and solution"},{"q":"Which sentence BEST states the main idea of *When the Earth Shakes*?","answer":"Earthquakes are caused by movements in the Earth's crust, and communities can prepare to reduce their effects.","explanation":"Main idea.","explanation_ar":"الفكرة الرئيسة.","options":["Al-Ais is a town in Saudi Arabia.","Seismometers record seismic waves.","Japan had an earthquake in 2011.","Earthquakes are caused by movements in the Earth's crust, and communities can prepare to reduce their effects."],"dok":3,"skill":"main idea"},{"q":"Why did the authorities evacuate residents near Al-Ais in 2009?","answer":"to keep them safe because the earthquakes were strong and continuing","explanation":"Inference.","explanation_ar":"لحمايتهم من الزلازل المستمرة.","options":["because a tsunami was coming","because of a wildfire","to keep them safe because the earthquakes were strong and continuing","because the town was being rebuilt"],"dok":3,"skill":"inference"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'crust': a cut-away of the Earth showing its thin outer layer"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'tectonic plate': a world map with plate boundaries drawn in red"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'fault': a crack in rock layers with arrows showing movement"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'seismic': wavy lines spreading out from a point underground"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'epicenter': a target symbol on a map above an underground star"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'magnitude': a scale from 1 to 9 with an arrow at 5.4"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'landslide': rocks sliding down a hillside"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS2-01","prompt":"The Earth's crust divided into tectonic plates."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS2-02","prompt":"A diagram of the focus, the epicenter and seismic waves."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS2-03","prompt":"Students practicing an earthquake drill."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V01","script":"crust. The Earth's crust is broken into plates."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V02","script":"tectonic plate. The Arabian Plate is moving away from Africa."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V03","script":"fault. The rocks slipped along a fault."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V04","script":"seismic. Seismic waves spread in every direction."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V05","script":"epicenter. The shaking was strongest at the epicenter."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V06","script":"magnitude. The earthquake had a magnitude of 5.4."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS2-V07","script":"landslide. The earthquake triggered a landslide in the mountains."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS2-T1","script":"On the evening of May 19, 2009, people in the town of Al-Ais, in western Saudi Arabia, felt the ground jolt beneath their feet. Walls cracked, and dishes rattled on their shelves. It was one of the strongest of more than 30,000 earthquakes that struck the area between April and June that year. Most were too small to feel, but this one measured 5.4. To keep people safe, the authorities evacuated about 40,000 residents until the shaking stopped. What causes the ground to shake like this, and what can people do about it? **A Planet in Pieces** – The Earth's outer layer, called the crust, is not one solid shell. It is broken into huge pieces called tectonic plates, which fit together like the pieces of a cracked eggshell. The plates move very slowly, usually a few centimeters a year, which is about as fast as your fingernails grow. The Arabian Plate, for example, is slowly moving away from Africa, and this movement is gradually widening the Red Sea. **How an Earthquake Happens** – Where two plates meet, their edges do not slide smoothly. They get stuck, and pressure builds up in the rocks for years or even centuries. When the pressure becomes too great, the rocks suddenly break and slip along a crack called a fault. The stored energy is released as seismic waves, which travel through the ground in every direction. The point underground where the rocks break is called the focus. The point on the surface directly above it is the epicenter, where the shaking is usually strongest. Earthquakes can also happen when hot melted rock pushes its way up through the crust, and scientists believe this is what happened near Al-Ais. **Measuring the Shaking** – Seismologists record earthquakes with instruments called seismometers. They describe the size of an earthquake with a number called its magnitude. Each step up the scale means a much bigger earthquake: a magnitude 6 earthquake releases about 32 times more energy than a magnitude 5. In Saudi Arabia, the Saudi Geological Survey runs a national network of seismic stations that monitors earthquakes day and night. **The Effects of Earthquakes** – Strong earthquakes can damage buildings, roads and bridges. In mountains, they can trigger landslides. When an earthquake happens under the ocean, it can push up the seafloor and create a tsunami, a series of giant waves that race toward the coast. In 2011, an earthquake of about magnitude 9 off the coast of Japan caused a tsunami that flooded large areas of land. **Problems and Solutions** – Scientists still cannot predict exactly when or where an earthquake will strike, so communities focus on being prepared. Engineers design buildings with strong, flexible frames that bend instead of breaking, and some buildings even stand on special pads that absorb the shaking. Early warning systems, such as the one in Japan, detect the first fast waves and send alerts a few seconds before the strongest shaking arrives. That is just enough time to stop trains and take cover. At school and at home, people practice a simple drill: drop to the ground, take cover under a sturdy table and hold on until the shaking stops. Earthquakes remind us that the ground beneath our feet is always moving. We cannot stop them, but with knowledge, strong buildings and good planning, communities can be ready when the Earth shakes."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS2-VID","script":"May 19, 2009. In Al-Ais, the ground jolts and walls crack. What makes the Earth shake? The crust is broken into plates that move about as fast as your fingernails grow… Stuck edges, building pressure, a sudden break along a fault – and seismic waves spread out. Seismometers, magnitude, early warnings, flexible buildings – and drop, cover, hold on! Headings, definitions, sequence signals – read science like a scientist!"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS3","title":"Cause/effect and problem/solution structures: \"Letter from a Relief Volunteer\"","lesson_type":"strategy_paired_text","objectives":["recognize cause/effect and problem/solution text structures and their signal words","trace chains of causes, effects, problems, solutions and results in a science article and a letter","compare how *When the Earth Shakes* and *Letter from a Relief Volunteer* show the effects of disasters"],"objectives_ar":["التعرف على بنيتي السبب والنتيجة والمشكلة والحل وكلماتهما الدالة.","تتبّع سلاسل الأسباب والنتائج والمشكلات والحلول في مقالة علمية ورسالة.","مقارنة طريقة عرض النصين لآثار الكوارث."],"success_criteria":["I can find four signal words for cause/effect and problem/solution in the texts.","I can complete a problem–solution–result chain for each problem in the letter.","I can explain one similarity and one difference between the two texts."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع إيجاد أربع كلمات دالة على السبب والنتيجة والمشكلة والحل.","أستطيع إكمال سلسلة مشكلة–حل–نتيجة لكل مشكلة في الرسالة.","أستطيع شرح وجه شبه ووجه اختلاف بين النصين."],"map_area":"Informational Text: Language, Craft, and Structure","vocabulary":[{"word":"cause","pos":"noun / verb","ipa":"/kɔz/","definition":"the reason something happens; to make something happen","arabic":"سبب / يسبّب","example":"The cause of the earthquake was moving rock.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"effect","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ɪˈfɛkt/","definition":"a change that happens because of something else","arabic":"نتيجة / أثر","example":"One effect of the earthquake was broken pipes.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"solution","pos":"noun","ipa":"/səˈluʃən/","definition":"a way to solve a problem","arabic":"حل","example":"Water tanks were a quick solution.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"signal word","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsɪɡnəl ˌwɝd/","definition":"a word that shows how ideas are connected, such as *because* or *as a result*","arabic":"كلمة دالة / مؤشر","example":"*As a result* is a signal word for an effect.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"valley","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈvæli/","definition":"low land between hills or mountains","arabic":"وادٍ / سهل بين الجبال","example":"The camp was in a valley.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"container","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kənˈteɪnər/","definition":"something such as a box or a bottle that is used to hold things","arabic":"وعاء / حاوية","example":"Families filled their water containers.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"rebuild","pos":"verb","ipa":"/riˈbɪld/","definition":"to build something again after it has been damaged","arabic":"يعيد البناء","example":"The villagers will rebuild their homes.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"Writers organize information in different **structures**. In a **cause/effect** structure, the writer explains **why** something happens (the **cause**) and **what happens as a result** (the **effect**). Signal words: ***because, since, so, as a result, therefore, due to, led to, caused, this means that***.\n\nIn a **problem/solution** structure, the writer describes a **problem**, explains a **solution** and often the **result**. Signal words: ***problem, difficulty, to solve this, one solution is, so that, as a result, now***. One text can use **both** structures, and effects often become **new problems**: *the earthquake broke the pipes (cause → effect) → there was no clean water (problem) → engineers set up tanks (solution) → families can fill containers (result)*.\n\nTo read these structures, **draw a chain**: cause → effect → problem → solution → result. Ask: *Why did this happen? What happened next? What was done about it? Did it work?* Then compare texts: a **science article** explains causes and effects in general, while a **personal letter** shows real problems and solutions through people's experiences.","examples":["Cause/effect (article): ***When the pressure becomes too great, the rocks suddenly break.***","Cause/effect (letter): ***Many houses were damaged, so hundreds of families could not go home.***","Signal word *because*: ***Because the earthquake broke the old water pipes, the villages had no clean water.***","Problem: ***The first problem was water.***","Solution: ***To solve this, engineers set up large water tanks and filters.***","Result: ***As a result, every family can now fill its containers twice a day.***","Problem → solution: ***Thin tents are not enough, so we gave out warm blankets and coats.***","Signal *That is why*: ***That is why we opened the tent school.***","Problem/solution in the article: ***Scientists cannot predict earthquakes, so communities focus on being prepared.***"],"common_errors":["✗ Mixing up cause and effect. → ✓ Ask: Which happened FIRST and made the other happen? (Cause comes first.)","✗ Listing solutions without the problem. → ✓ Name the problem, then the solution and the result. (Complete chains.)","✗ Missing signal words like *so* and *that is why*. → ✓ Short words can signal effects too. (Notice small signals.)","✗ The earthquake was the effect of the broken pipes. → ✓ The broken pipes were an effect of the earthquake. (Check the order.)","✗ Because the pipes broke. The villages had no water. → ✓ Because the pipes broke, the villages had no water. (*Because* clause + comma + main clause.)","✗ Thinking a letter cannot have a structure. → ✓ Letters can use problem/solution too. (Any genre.)","✗ As a result families can now fill containers. → ✓ As a result, families can now fill containers. (Comma after *As a result*.)"],"arabic":"ينظّم الكتّاب المعلومات في بنى مختلفة. في بنية السبب والنتيجة يشرح الكاتب لماذا يحدث الشيء وماذا يحدث نتيجة له، ومن كلماتها: because وso وas a result وtherefore وdue to وled to.\n\nوفي بنية المشكلة والحل يصف الكاتب مشكلة ثم يشرح الحل وغالبًا النتيجة، ومن كلماتها: problem وto solve this وone solution is وso that. وقد يجمع نص واحد البنيتين، وكثيرًا ما تصبح النتيجة مشكلة جديدة.\n\nلقراءة هذه البنى ارسم سلسلة: سبب ← نتيجة ← مشكلة ← حل ← أثر الحل. واسأل: لماذا حدث؟ وماذا حدث بعده؟ وماذا فُعل؟ وهل نجح؟ والمقالة العلمية تشرح الأسباب والنتائج عمومًا، أما الرسالة الشخصية فتُظهر المشكلات والحلول من خلال تجارب الناس.\n\nانتبه: ضع فاصلة بعد جملة because إذا جاءت في البداية، وبعد As a result."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"Dear Grade 7 students, thank you for your letters and drawings…","on_screen":"Letter from a Relief Volunteer ✉️","visual":"A volunteer in a camp pinning children's drawings to the wall of a tent school."},{"time":"00:30–01:40","narration":"Water, cold, school – three problems, three solutions.","on_screen":"Problem → Solution → Result","visual":"Water tanks being filled; blankets handed out; children learning in a tent."},{"time":"01:40–02:40","narration":"Because the earthquake broke the pipes, the villages had no clean water. As a result… That is why…","on_screen":"Signal words","visual":"Signal words light up like traffic signals."},{"time":"02:40–03:30","narration":"The article explains why earthquakes happen. The letter shows how people solve the problems they cause.","on_screen":"Comparing two texts","visual":"The article and the letter side by side, connected by a chain."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"We will build it again – stronger.","on_screen":"A community that is ready can save many lives","visual":"A child's drawing of a village with new houses and a rainbow."}],"captions_ar":["أعزائي طلاب الصف السابع، شكرًا لرسائلكم ورسوماتكم…","الماء والبرد والمدرسة: ثلاث مشكلات وثلاثة حلول.","لأن الزلزال كسر الأنابيب، لم يكن في القرى ماء نظيف. ونتيجة لذلك… ولهذا…","المقالة تشرح لماذا تحدث الزلازل، والرسالة تُظهر كيف يحل الناس المشكلات التي تسببها.","سنبنيها من جديد، أقوى."],"voice":"Narrator: American English, warm male voice for Mr. Saad; clear female voice for the explanation; speed 1.0."},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Letter from a Relief Volunteer","body":"Dear Grade 7 students,\n\nThank you for your letters and drawings. They arrived yesterday, and I pinned them to the wall of our tent school, where they make everyone smile.\n\nYou asked what my work is like here. Three weeks ago, a strong earthquake hit these mountain villages. Many houses were damaged, so hundreds of families could not go home. Our relief team was sent to help in a camp in the valley.\n\nEvery day brings a new problem to solve. The first problem was water. Because the earthquake broke the old water pipes, the villages had no clean water, and people could become sick from drinking unsafe water. To solve this, engineers set up large water tanks and filters. As a result, every family can now fill its containers twice a day.\n\nThe second problem was the cold. At night, the temperature in the mountains drops below zero. Thin tents are not enough, so we gave out warm blankets and coats. Now, the engineers are building small insulated cabins, which will keep families warm until their homes are rebuilt.\n\nThe problem that worried me most was the children. Their school was badly damaged, and many of them were frightened and bored. That is why we opened the tent school. Teachers from the villages teach reading and math in the mornings, and in the afternoons the children play football and draw. Last week, a ten-year-old girl named Leen drew a picture of her village with new houses and a rainbow. She told me, \"We will build it again – stronger.\"\n\nYou asked how you can help. Please ask your families to give only through official charities, so that help reaches the right people. Most of all, keep learning about how to prepare for emergencies. A community that is ready can save many lives.\n\nWith warm wishes from the mountains,\n\nMr. Saad Al-Otaibi\n\nRelief volunteer and your former science teacher","word_count":318,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS3-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L07-U4-LS3-1","genre":"letter/email","role":"paired","difficulty":"800L–950L","glossary":[{"word":"insulated","definition":"covered with material that keeps heat in","arabic":"معزول حراريًا"},{"word":"filter","definition":"a device that removes dirt from water or air","arabic":"مُرشِّح / فلتر"},{"word":"charity","definition":"an organization that collects money and gifts to help people in need","arabic":"جمعية خيرية"}]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the letter. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ الرسالة واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"Where did Mr. Saad pin the students' letters and drawings?","answer":"on the wall of the tent school","explanation":"Paragraph 2.","explanation_ar":"على جدار مدرسة الخيمة.","options":["in his car","at the water tanks","on the wall of the tent school","in a hospital"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How long ago did the earthquake hit the mountain villages?","answer":"three weeks ago","explanation":"Paragraph 3.","explanation_ar":"قبل ثلاثة أسابيع.","options":["three years ago","three weeks ago","yesterday","three months ago"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How many times a day can each family now fill its water containers?","answer":"twice","explanation":"Paragraph 4.","explanation_ar":"مرتين.","options":["once","three times","only once a week","twice"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What are the engineers building to keep families warm?","answer":"small insulated cabins","explanation":"Paragraph 5.","explanation_ar":"كبائن صغيرة معزولة.","options":["a new hospital","small insulated cabins","a football stadium","a bridge"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What did Leen draw?","answer":"her village with new houses and a rainbow","explanation":"Paragraph 6.","explanation_ar":"قريتها بمنازل جديدة وقوس قزح.","options":["her village with new houses and a rainbow","a water tank","Mr. Saad's tent","a map of the valley"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How does Mr. Saad ask the students' families to help?","answer":"by giving only through official charities","explanation":"Paragraph 7.","explanation_ar":"بالتبرع عبر الجمعيات الرسمية.","options":["by giving only through official charities","by traveling to the camp","by sending money directly to him","by writing to the newspaper"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS3-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Cause/effect and problem/solution. Choose.","instructions_ar":"السبب والنتيجة والمشكلة والحل: اختر.","items":[{"q":"In the letter, what CAUSED the villages to have no clean water?","answer":"The earthquake broke the old water pipes.","explanation":"Cause.","explanation_ar":"انكسار أنابيب المياه.","options":["The earthquake broke the old water pipes.","The engineers built cabins.","The temperature dropped below zero.","The children were bored."],"dok":2,"skill":"cause and effect","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which signal word introduces a SOLUTION in *To solve this, engineers set up large water tanks and filters*?","answer":"To solve this","explanation":"Signal words.","explanation_ar":"To solve this.","options":["engineers","To solve this","large","and"],"dok":2,"skill":"signal words","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What problem did the tent school solve?","answer":"The children's school was damaged, and they were frightened and bored.","explanation":"Problem/solution.","explanation_ar":"تضرر المدرسة وخوف الأطفال وملَلهم.","options":["The families had no blankets.","The water pipes were broken.","The children's school was damaged, and they were frightened and bored.","The roads were blocked."],"dok":2,"skill":"problem and solution","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence from the article shows a cause/effect structure?","answer":"When the pressure becomes too great, the rocks suddenly break.","explanation":"Structure.","explanation_ar":"سبب ونتيجة.","options":["The Earth's outer layer is called the crust.","When the pressure becomes too great, the rocks suddenly break.","Seismologists record earthquakes.","The Arabian Plate is a tectonic plate."],"dok":2,"skill":"text structure","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about the relief camp is punctuated correctly?","answer":"Because the nights were cold, the team gave out blankets.","explanation":"Punctuation.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Because the nights were cold the team gave out blankets.","Because, the nights were cold the team gave out blankets.","Because the nights were cold. The team gave out blankets.","Because the nights were cold, the team gave out blankets."],"dok":2,"skill":"punctuation","stage":"guided"},{"q":"How are the two texts DIFFERENT in the way they show earthquakes?","answer":"The article explains causes and effects scientifically; the letter shows real problems and solutions through people's experiences.","explanation":"Comparing texts.","explanation_ar":"العلم مقابل التجربة الإنسانية.","options":["Both only list scientific facts.","Both are personal letters.","The article explains causes and effects scientifically; the letter shows real problems and solutions through people's experiences.","The article tells a story about Leen."],"dok":3,"skill":"comparing texts","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS3-A3","type":"organizer","stage":"freer","instructions":"Organizer: Complete the chains.","instructions_ar":"أكمل السلاسل.","items":[{"q":"Chain 2 – the cold: problem, solution and result.","answer":"Problem: nights drop below zero and thin tents are not enough. Solution: warm blankets and coats; insulated cabins. Result: families will stay warm until their homes are rebuilt.","explanation":"Chain.","explanation_ar":"السلسلة.","dok":2,"skill":"problem and solution","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Chain 3 – the children: problem, solution and result.","answer":"Problem: the school was badly damaged and children were frightened and bored. Solution: a tent school with lessons and play. Result: children learn, play and feel hopeful, like Leen.","explanation":"Chain.","explanation_ar":"السلسلة.","dok":2,"skill":"problem and solution","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Write one cause/effect chain from *When the Earth Shakes*.","answer":"An earthquake happens under the ocean (cause) → the seafloor is pushed up (effect) → a tsunami forms and races toward the coast (further effect).","explanation":"Cause/effect.","explanation_ar":"السبب والنتيجة.","dok":2,"skill":"cause and effect","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Write four signal words from the texts and say whether each shows cause/effect or problem/solution.","answer":"because (cause/effect), as a result (effect), to solve this (solution), that is why (effect / reason for a solution)","explanation":"Signal words.","explanation_ar":"الكلمات الدالة.","dok":2,"skill":"signal words","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS3-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Explain and compare.","instructions_ar":"اشرح وقارن.","items":[{"q":"Explain how one effect of the earthquake became a new problem in the camp.","answer":"The earthquake damaged many houses, so families could not go home. This created a new problem: they needed shelter in the cold mountains.","explanation":"Effects → problems.","explanation_ar":"النتائج تصبح مشكلات.","dok":3,"skill":"cause and effect","stage":"production"},{"q":"What does Leen's sentence *We will build it again – stronger* show about her?","answer":"It shows that she is hopeful and determined, and that she has learned that rebuilding can make the village safer.","explanation":"Inference.","explanation_ar":"الاستنتاج.","dok":3,"skill":"inference","stage":"production"},{"q":"Compare the two texts: one similarity and one difference.","answer":"Both show that preparation and planning help people survive disasters. However, the article gives scientific explanations, while the letter describes real people and their daily problems.","explanation":"Compare.","explanation_ar":"قارن.","dok":3,"skill":"comparing texts","stage":"production"},{"q":"Why does Mr. Saad ask families to give only through official charities?","answer":"Because official charities make sure that help reaches the right people and is used safely.","explanation":"Inference.","explanation_ar":"الاستنتاج.","dok":2,"skill":"inference","stage":"production"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS3-TR-1","type":"timed_reading","instructions":"Timed reading: Read \"Letter from a Relief Volunteer\" aloud or silently. Try to finish in the time.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص وحاول أن تنهيه في الوقت المحدد.","items":[{"q":"Read “Letter from a Relief Volunteer”.","answer":"318 words","title":"Letter from a Relief Volunteer","body":"Dear Grade 7 students,\n\nThank you for your letters and drawings. They arrived yesterday, and I pinned them to the wall of our tent school, where they make everyone smile.\n\nYou asked what my work is like here. Three weeks ago, a strong earthquake hit these mountain villages. Many houses were damaged, so hundreds of families could not go home. Our relief team was sent to help in a camp in the valley.\n\nEvery day brings a new problem to solve. The first problem was water. Because the earthquake broke the old water pipes, the villages had no clean water, and people could become sick from drinking unsafe water. To solve this, engineers set up large water tanks and filters. As a result, every family can now fill its containers twice a day.\n\nThe second problem was the cold. At night, the temperature in the mountains drops below zero. Thin tents are not enough, so we gave out warm blankets and coats. Now, the engineers are building small insulated cabins, which will keep families warm until their homes are rebuilt.\n\nThe problem that worried me most was the children. Their school was badly damaged, and many of them were frightened and bored. That is why we opened the tent school. Teachers from the villages teach reading and math in the mornings, and in the afternoons the children play football and draw. Last week, a ten-year-old girl named Leen drew a picture of her village with new houses and a rainbow. She told me, \"We will build it again – stronger.\"\n\nYou asked how you can help. Please ask your families to give only through official charities, so that help reaches the right people. Most of all, keep learning about how to prepare for emergencies. A community that is ready can save many lives.\n\nWith warm wishes from the mountains,\n\nMr. Saad Al-Otaibi\n\nRelief volunteer and your former science teacher","words":318,"limit":161}],"time_limit_sec":161},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS3-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Cause–Problem–Solution Chain","instructions":"Organizer: Use both texts. Fill in each link of the chain and write the signal words you found.","items":[{"q":"Cause (article or letter)","answer":"The earthquake broke the old water pipes."},{"q":"Effect","answer":"The villages had no clean water."},{"q":"Problem it created","answer":"People could become sick from unsafe water."},{"q":"Solution","answer":"Engineers set up large water tanks and filters."},{"q":"Result","answer":"Every family can fill its containers twice a day."},{"q":"Signal words I found","answer":"because, so, to solve this, as a result, that is why, when"}]}],"game":{"name":"Domino Chains","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Domino run: the child places domino cards in the correct order and drops a signal-word connector between each pair; when the chain is correct, the dominoes fall in an animation and the camp lights up.","Each team receives mixed domino cards for one chain.","They arrange the dominoes in order on the table.","They place a correct signal-word connector between each pair.","They read the chain aloud as full sentences.","Correct order = 2 points; each correct signal word = 1 point."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"In a cause/effect text, what is an *effect*?","answer":"a change that happens because of something else","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"نتيجة.","options":["the reason something happens","a way to solve a problem","a change that happens because of something else","a kind of letter"],"dok":1,"skill":"cause and effect"},{"q":"Which phrase is a signal word for an EFFECT?","answer":"As a result,","explanation":"Signal words.","explanation_ar":"As a result.","options":["Because","As a result,","The problem was","Dear"],"dok":1,"skill":"signal words"},{"q":"*The camp was in a **valley**.* *Valley* means ___.","answer":"low land between hills or mountains","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"وادٍ.","options":["a tall mountain","a deep ocean","a busy city","low land between hills or mountains"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which text structure does *The first problem was water. To solve this, engineers set up tanks* use?","answer":"problem/solution","explanation":"Structure.","explanation_ar":"مشكلة وحل.","options":["chronological order only","compare/contrast","description only","problem/solution"],"dok":1,"skill":"text structure"},{"q":"Which sentence about the earthquake is punctuated correctly?","answer":"As a result, many families needed shelter.","explanation":"Comma.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["As a result, many families needed shelter.","As a result many families, needed shelter.","As, a result many families needed shelter.","As a result many families needed, shelter."],"dok":2,"skill":"punctuation"},{"q":"In *Many houses were damaged, so hundreds of families could not go home*, what is the CAUSE?","answer":"Many houses were damaged.","explanation":"Cause.","explanation_ar":"تضرر المنازل.","options":["Many houses were damaged.","Hundreds of families could not go home.","so","families"],"dok":2,"skill":"cause and effect"},{"q":"Why did the relief team open a tent school?","answer":"because the children's school was badly damaged and they needed to learn and feel safe","explanation":"Problem/solution.","explanation_ar":"لأن المدرسة تضررت.","options":["because the teachers wanted a holiday","because the children's school was badly damaged and they needed to learn and feel safe","because it was cheaper than water tanks","because the children asked for football"],"dok":2,"skill":"problem and solution"},{"q":"What is the purpose of Mr. Saad's letter?","answer":"to thank the students, describe the relief work and suggest how they can help","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"الشكر والوصف واقتراح المساعدة.","options":["to thank the students, describe the relief work and suggest how they can help","to sell blankets","to explain how tectonic plates move","to complain about the students' drawings"],"dok":2,"skill":"author's purpose"},{"q":"How do the article and the letter work TOGETHER to answer the unit question?","answer":"The article explains why earthquakes happen and how to prepare; the letter shows how communities recover afterward.","explanation":"Synthesis.","explanation_ar":"يتكاملان.","options":["Both describe only the science of plates.","Both are about wildfires.","Neither mentions solutions.","The article explains why earthquakes happen and how to prepare; the letter shows how communities recover afterward."],"dok":3,"skill":"comparing texts"},{"q":"Which effect in the letter becomes a NEW problem?","answer":"Damaged houses meant families had to live in thin tents in the cold.","explanation":"Chains.","explanation_ar":"النتيجة تصبح مشكلة.","options":["The children drew pictures.","The letters arrived yesterday.","Damaged houses meant families had to live in thin tents in the cold.","Mr. Saad was a science teacher."],"dok":3,"skill":"cause and effect"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'cause': a domino about to knock over another"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'effect': dominoes falling in a row"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'solution': a key fitting into a lock"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'signal word': a traffic signal with words instead of lights"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'valley': tents in a green valley between mountains"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'container': plastic water containers lined up"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'rebuild': workers building a new house"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS3-01","prompt":"A tent school in a relief camp."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS3-02","prompt":"Clean water tanks in a relief camp."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS3-03","prompt":"A child's hopeful drawing of a rebuilt village."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V01","script":"cause. The cause of the earthquake was moving rock."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V02","script":"effect. One effect of the earthquake was broken pipes."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V03","script":"solution. Water tanks were a quick solution."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V04","script":"signal word. *As a result* is a signal word for an effect."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V05","script":"valley. The camp was in a valley."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V06","script":"container. Families filled their water containers."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS3-V07","script":"rebuild. The villagers will rebuild their homes."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS3-T1","script":"Dear Grade 7 students, Thank you for your letters and drawings. They arrived yesterday, and I pinned them to the wall of our tent school, where they make everyone smile. You asked what my work is like here. Three weeks ago, a strong earthquake hit these mountain villages. Many houses were damaged, so hundreds of families could not go home. Our relief team was sent to help in a camp in the valley. Every day brings a new problem to solve. The first problem was water. Because the earthquake broke the old water pipes, the villages had no clean water, and people could become sick from drinking unsafe water. To solve this, engineers set up large water tanks and filters. As a result, every family can now fill its containers twice a day. The second problem was the cold. At night, the temperature in the mountains drops below zero. Thin tents are not enough, so we gave out warm blankets and coats. Now, the engineers are building small insulated cabins, which will keep families warm until their homes are rebuilt. The problem that worried me most was the children. Their school was badly damaged, and many of them were frightened and bored. That is why we opened the tent school. Teachers from the villages teach reading and math in the mornings, and in the afternoons the children play football and draw. Last week, a ten-year-old girl named Leen drew a picture of her village with new houses and a rainbow. She told me, \"We will build it again – stronger.\" You asked how you can help. Please ask your families to give only through official charities, so that help reaches the right people. Most of all, keep learning about how to prepare for emergencies. A community that is ready can save many lives. With warm wishes from the mountains, Mr. Saad Al-Otaibi Relief volunteer and your former science teacher"},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS3-VID","script":"Dear Grade 7 students, thank you for your letters and drawings… Water, cold, school – three problems, three solutions. Because the earthquake broke the pipes, the villages had no clean water. As a result… That is why… The article explains why earthquakes happen. The letter shows how people solve the problems they cause. We will build it again – stronger."}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS4","title":"Writing Skill: Report sections with headings","lesson_type":"writing_skill","objectives":["identify the features of an informational report: title, introduction, headings, sections, topic sentences and conclusion","write clear headings and a topic sentence for each section","group facts into the right sections and keep an objective, third-person tone"],"objectives_ar":["تحديد سمات التقرير المعلوماتي: العنوان والمقدمة والعناوين الفرعية والأقسام والجمل الافتتاحية والخاتمة.","كتابة عناوين واضحة وجملة افتتاحية لكل قسم.","تجميع المعلومات في الأقسام المناسبة والحفاظ على أسلوب موضوعي بضمير الغائب."],"success_criteria":["I can label the parts of Noura's report.","I can write a heading that tells readers exactly what a section is about.","I can sort facts into sections and write a topic sentence for each one."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع تسمية أجزاء تقرير نورة.","أستطيع كتابة عنوان يخبر القارئ بمحتوى القسم بدقة.","أستطيع فرز المعلومات في أقسام وكتابة جملة افتتاحية لكل قسم."],"map_area":"Language Usage: Write, Revise Texts for Purpose and Audience (cross-strand)","vocabulary":[{"word":"report","pos":"noun","ipa":"/rɪˈpɔrt/","definition":"a piece of writing that gives organized facts about a topic","arabic":"تقرير","example":"Noura wrote a report about flash floods.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"heading","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈhɛdɪŋ/","definition":"a short title at the start of a section of writing","arabic":"عنوان فرعي","example":"Each heading tells you what the section is about.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"section","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsɛkʃən/","definition":"one of the parts that something is divided into","arabic":"قسم","example":"The report has five sections.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"topic sentence","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈtɑpɪk ˌsɛntəns/","definition":"a sentence that gives the main idea of a paragraph or a section","arabic":"الجملة الافتتاحية / جملة الفكرة الرئيسة","example":"Start each section with a topic sentence.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"flash flood","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌflæʃ ˈflʌd/","definition":"a flood that happens very quickly after heavy rain","arabic":"سيل مفاجئ / فيضان خاطف","example":"A flash flood filled the wadi in minutes.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"precaution","pos":"noun","ipa":"/prɪˈkɔʃən/","definition":"something you do to prevent danger before it happens","arabic":"احتياط / تدبير وقائي","example":"An emergency kit is a useful precaution.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"third person","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌθɝd ˈpɝsən/","definition":"writing that uses *he, she, it, they* or nouns, not *I* or *you*","arabic":"ضمير الغائب","example":"Reports are usually written in the third person.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"An **informational report** gives **organized facts** about a topic. Readers often **skim** reports to find information quickly, so reports use a clear **title** and **headings** that divide the information into **sections**. Each section answers **one question** about the topic.\n\nStructure: **Introduction** (a hook + what the report will cover) → **body sections** with headings (for example *What Is…?*, *Why…?*, *How to…*) → **Conclusion** (the main idea and a final message, **no new facts**). Begin each section with a **topic sentence** that states its main idea, then give **facts, definitions, examples** and **numbers** with their **sources**.\n\nGood headings are **short**, **specific** and **parallel** (similar in form): *What Is a Flash Flood? / Why Are Flash Floods Dangerous? / How to Stay Safe*. Keep the tone **objective** and use the **third person** (*families should…*), not *I think* or *you guys*. Before writing, **sort your notes** into piles – one pile for each heading.","examples":["Title: ***Flash Floods: Be Prepared***","Introduction (purpose): ***This report explains what flash floods are, why they are dangerous and how families can stay safe.***","Heading as a question: ***What Is a Flash Flood?***","Topic sentence (definition): ***A flash flood is a flood that develops very quickly.***","Topic sentence (main idea): ***Moving water is much more powerful than it looks.***","Fact with a source: ***According to the US National Weather Service, just 15 centimeters of fast-moving water can knock an adult down.***","Advice in the third person: ***Families should follow warnings from the National Center for Meteorology.***","Parallel headings: ***What Is…? / Why Are…? / How to…***","Conclusion: ***By understanding the danger and preparing early, families can protect themselves.***"],"common_errors":["✗ Headings that are too general: *Information*, *Stuff*. → ✓ Specific headings: *How to Stay Safe*. (Be specific.)","✗ Putting a fact in the wrong section. → ✓ Sort notes by heading before you write. (Group facts.)","✗ Starting a section without a topic sentence. → ✓ Begin with the main idea of the section. (Topic sentence first.)","✗ I think floods are super scary, guys! → ✓ Flash floods can be very dangerous. (Objective, third person.)","✗ Adding new facts in the conclusion. → ✓ Sum up; don't add new information. (Conclusion = summary.)","✗ Flash floods develops quickly. → ✓ Flash floods develop quickly. (Plural subject: **develop**.)","✗ Headings in different forms: *What Is a Flood? / Danger / You Must Be Safe*. → ✓ Make them parallel: *What Is…? / Why…? / How to…* (Parallel headings.)"],"arabic":"التقرير المعلوماتي يقدّم حقائق منظمة عن موضوع، وكثيرًا ما يتصفحه القارئ بسرعة، لذلك يستخدم عنوانًا رئيسًا واضحًا وعناوين فرعية تقسم المعلومات إلى أقسام يجيب كل منها عن سؤال واحد.\n\nالبناء: مقدمة (افتتاحية وما سيتناوله التقرير)، ثم أقسام بعناوين فرعية، ثم خاتمة (الفكرة الرئيسة ورسالة أخيرة دون معلومات جديدة). ابدأ كل قسم بجملة افتتاحية تذكر فكرته الرئيسة، ثم قدّم الحقائق والتعريفات والأمثلة والأرقام مع مصادرها.\n\nالعناوين الجيدة قصيرة ومحددة ومتوازية في صيغتها، والأسلوب موضوعي بضمير الغائب. وقبل الكتابة افرز ملاحظاتك في مجموعات، مجموعة لكل عنوان.\n\nانتبه: لا تضف معلومات جديدة في الخاتمة."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"Floods in the desert? Yes! And a good report can help families stay safe.","on_screen":"Report Sections with Headings 📑","visual":"A dry wadi under dark clouds; a student opening a notebook."},{"time":"00:30–01:30","narration":"Introduction. What Is a Flash Flood? Why Are Flash Floods Dangerous? How to Stay Safe. Conclusion.","on_screen":"(model report)","visual":"Each heading appears as a labeled folder."},{"time":"01:30–02:40","narration":"Each heading answers one question. Each section starts with a topic sentence.","on_screen":"Headings + topic sentences","visual":"Sticky notes being sorted into piles under each heading."},{"time":"02:40–03:30","narration":"Facts with sources, third person, objective tone – no 'I think' and no 'you guys'.","on_screen":"Objective tone ✔","visual":"'I think floods are super scary!' changes into 'Flash floods can be very dangerous.'"},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"A clear report helps readers find what they need – fast!","on_screen":"Skim-friendly writing","visual":"A reader quickly finds the 'How to Stay Safe' section."}],"captions_ar":["فيضانات في الصحراء؟ نعم! والتقرير الجيد يساعد الأسر على البقاء آمنة.","المقدمة، ما السيل المفاجئ؟ لماذا السيول خطرة؟ كيف تبقى آمنًا؟ الخاتمة.","كل عنوان يجيب عن سؤال واحد، وكل قسم يبدأ بجملة افتتاحية.","حقائق بمصادرها، وضمير الغائب، وأسلوب موضوعي، دون «أظن» أو «يا شباب».","التقرير الواضح يساعد القارئ على إيجاد ما يحتاج إليه بسرعة!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, clear, friendly female voice; Noura reads her report; speed 1.0."},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Flash Floods: Be Prepared (a report by Noura)","body":"**Introduction** – Saudi Arabia is famous for its deserts, so many people are surprised to learn that floods are one of its most serious natural hazards. This report explains what flash floods are, why they are dangerous and how families can stay safe.\n\n**What Is a Flash Flood?** – A flash flood is a flood that develops very quickly, often within minutes or a few hours of heavy rain. In dry areas, the hard ground cannot absorb much water, so rain runs off quickly into wadis and streets. A dry wadi can become a fast river in a very short time.\n\n**Why Are Flash Floods Dangerous?** – Moving water is much more powerful than it looks. According to the US National Weather Service, just 15 centimeters of fast-moving water can knock an adult down, and about 30 centimeters can carry away a small car. Floodwater may also hide holes, broken roads and fallen power lines.\n\n**How to Stay Safe** – Families should follow warnings from Saudi Arabia's National Center for Meteorology and the civil defense. During heavy rain, people should stay away from wadis and low roads, never walk or drive through moving water and move to higher ground if water rises. A home emergency kit with water, a flashlight and a first-aid kit is a useful precaution.\n\n**Conclusion** – Flash floods are rare in many places, but they can be deadly when they happen. By understanding the danger and preparing early, families can protect themselves.","word_count":241,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS4-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L07-U4-LS4-1","genre":"report","role":"model","difficulty":"800L–950L","glossary":[],"annotations":["**Title** names the topic and the purpose: *Flash Floods: Be Prepared*.","**Introduction:** a hook (*famous for its deserts*) and a sentence that tells readers what the report will cover.","**Headings** divide the report into **sections**; each heading tells the reader exactly what the section is about.","**Topic sentences** begin each section: *A flash flood is…*, *Moving water is much more powerful than it looks.*","**Facts with sources:** *According to the US National Weather Service, …*","**Objective, third-person tone:** *families should…*, not *I think…* or *you guys*.","**Conclusion:** sums up the main idea and gives a final message, with no new facts."]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read Noura's report. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ تقرير نورة واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"Why are many people surprised by floods in Saudi Arabia, according to Noura's introduction?","answer":"Saudi Arabia is famous for its deserts.","explanation":"Introduction.","explanation_ar":"لأنها مشهورة بالصحارى.","options":["It never rains anywhere in the world.","Floods only happen at night.","Saudi Arabia is famous for its deserts.","There are no wadis in Saudi Arabia."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Why does rain run off quickly in dry areas?","answer":"The hard ground cannot absorb much water.","explanation":"What Is a Flash Flood?","explanation_ar":"الأرض الصلبة لا تمتص الماء.","options":["The hard ground cannot absorb much water.","The sand is too soft.","The wind blows the water away.","The rain is too warm."],"dok":1,"skill":"cause and effect","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"According to the US National Weather Service, how much fast-moving water can knock an adult down?","answer":"just 15 centimeters","explanation":"Why Are Flash Floods Dangerous?","explanation_ar":"15 سنتيمترًا.","options":["about 2 meters","about 1 centimeter","about 5 meters","just 15 centimeters"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What can floodwater hide, according to the report?","answer":"holes, broken roads and fallen power lines","explanation":"Why Are Flash Floods Dangerous?","explanation_ar":"حفر وطرق مكسورة.","options":["sand dunes","mountains","water tanks","holes, broken roads and fallen power lines"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Whose warnings should families follow, according to Noura?","answer":"the National Center for Meteorology and the civil defense","explanation":"How to Stay Safe.","explanation_ar":"المركز الوطني للأرصاد والدفاع المدني.","options":["the National Center for Meteorology and the civil defense","social media rumors","their friends only","a cartoon channel"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which section of Noura's report would you read to find out what to put in an emergency kit?","answer":"How to Stay Safe","explanation":"Headings.","explanation_ar":"كيف تبقى آمنًا.","options":["Introduction","How to Stay Safe","What Is a Flash Flood?","Why Are Flash Floods Dangerous?"],"dok":2,"skill":"text features","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS4-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Headings, sections and tone. Choose.","instructions_ar":"العناوين والأقسام والأسلوب: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which is the BEST heading for a section about the causes of sandstorms?","answer":"What Causes Sandstorms?","explanation":"Specific heading.","explanation_ar":"أفضل عنوان.","options":["Stuff About Sand","Sandstorms Are So Annoying!","Section 2","What Causes Sandstorms?"],"dok":2,"skill":"headings","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Under which heading does this fact belong: *Sandstorms can reduce visibility on highways to a few meters*?","answer":"Why Are Sandstorms Dangerous?","explanation":"Sorting facts.","explanation_ar":"لماذا العواصف الرملية خطرة؟","options":["What Causes Sandstorms?","Introduction","Why Are Sandstorms Dangerous?","Sources"],"dok":2,"skill":"organizing","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which is the BEST topic sentence for a section called *How to Stay Safe in a Heat Wave*?","answer":"There are several simple ways to stay safe during a heat wave.","explanation":"Topic sentence.","explanation_ar":"أفضل جملة افتتاحية.","options":["I hate hot weather.","There are several simple ways to stay safe during a heat wave.","The sun is a star.","Heat waves happen in summer, you know."],"dok":2,"skill":"topic sentences","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence keeps an OBJECTIVE, third-person tone?","answer":"People should drink plenty of water during a heat wave.","explanation":"Tone.","explanation_ar":"أسلوب موضوعي.","options":["I think heat waves are the worst thing ever.","People should drink plenty of water during a heat wave.","You guys better drink water!","Honestly, I'm always thirsty."],"dok":2,"skill":"formal tone","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about flash floods is correct?","answer":"Flash floods develop very quickly after heavy rain.","explanation":"Agreement.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Flash floods develops very quickly after heavy rain.","Flash floods developing very quickly after heavy rain.","Flash floods develop very quickly after heavy rain.","Flash floods is develop very quickly after heavy rain."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which set of headings is PARALLEL?","answer":"What Is a Heat Wave? / Why Are Heat Waves Dangerous? / How to Stay Cool","explanation":"Parallel headings.","explanation_ar":"عناوين متوازية.","options":["What Is a Heat Wave? / Why Are Heat Waves Dangerous? / How to Stay Cool","Heat Wave / Danger! / You Must Stay Cool","Intro / Some Facts / Other Facts","Hot / Hotter / Hottest"],"dok":3,"skill":"headings","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS4-A3","type":"gap_fill","stage":"freer","instructions":"Freer practice: Complete the report tips with the word bank.","instructions_ar":"أكمل نصائح التقرير من بنك الكلمات.","items":[{"q":"A ________ tells readers exactly what a section is about.","answer":"heading","explanation":"Text feature.","explanation_ar":"العنوان الفرعي.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Begin every section with a ________ sentence that gives its main idea.","answer":"topic","explanation":"Structure.","explanation_ar":"افتتاحية.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Write reports in the ________ person: he, she, it, they.","answer":"third","explanation":"Tone.","explanation_ar":"الغائب.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Keeping a first-aid kit at home is a sensible ________.","answer":"precaution","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"احتياط.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"The ________ sums up the main idea and does not add new facts.","answer":"conclusion","explanation":"Structure.","explanation_ar":"الخاتمة.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"A report about storms might have one ________ on causes and one on safety.","answer":"section","explanation":"Structure.","explanation_ar":"قسم.","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS4-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Plan report sections.","instructions_ar":"خطط لأقسام التقرير.","items":[{"q":"Write three parallel headings for a report about sandstorms.","answer":"What Is a Sandstorm? / Why Are Sandstorms Dangerous? / How to Stay Safe in a Sandstorm","explanation":"Headings.","explanation_ar":"العناوين.","dok":2,"skill":"headings","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a topic sentence for each of your three headings.","answer":"A sandstorm is a strong wind that carries large amounts of sand and dust. Sandstorms can be dangerous because they make it hard to see and breathe. There are several ways to stay safe when a sandstorm arrives.","explanation":"Topic sentences.","explanation_ar":"الجمل الافتتاحية.","dok":2,"skill":"topic sentences","stage":"production"},{"q":"Rewrite in an objective, third-person tone: *You guys should totally stay inside when the sand is crazy!*","answer":"People should stay indoors when a sandstorm is strong.","explanation":"Tone.","explanation_ar":"الأسلوب.","dok":2,"skill":"formal tone","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write an introduction for a report about sandstorms (two sentences).","answer":"Sandstorms are common in many parts of Saudi Arabia, especially in spring. This report explains what sandstorms are, why they are dangerous and how people can stay safe.","explanation":"Introduction.","explanation_ar":"المقدمة.","dok":3,"skill":"introductions","stage":"production"}]}],"game":{"name":"Report Sorting Station","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Sorting station: facts slide down a conveyor belt; the child drops each fact into the correct heading folder. Then the child picks the best topic sentence for each folder to 'publish' the report.","Each team chooses a hazard and receives twelve mixed fact cards.","The team writes three parallel headings on folders.","They sort the facts into the folders.","They choose or write a topic sentence for each folder.","Correctly sorted fact = 1 point; parallel headings = 3 points; strong topic sentences = 1 point each."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is a *heading* in a report?","answer":"a short title at the start of a section","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"عنوان فرعي.","options":["the last sentence of the report","a short title at the start of a section","a list of sources","a picture of the author"],"dok":1,"skill":"headings"},{"q":"What is a *flash flood*?","answer":"a flood that happens very quickly after heavy rain","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"سيل مفاجئ.","options":["a slow, gentle river","a flood that happens very quickly after heavy rain","a kind of camera light","a flood that lasts for years"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*Keeping a flashlight at home is a useful **precaution**.* *Precaution* means ___.","answer":"something you do to prevent danger","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"احتياط.","options":["a kind of storm","a type of report","a mistake","something you do to prevent danger"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Where should a topic sentence usually go?","answer":"at the beginning of a section","explanation":"Structure.","explanation_ar":"في بداية القسم.","options":["at the beginning of a section","only in the title","after the conclusion","in the sources list"],"dok":1,"skill":"topic sentences"},{"q":"Which sentence about sandstorms is correct?","answer":"Sandstorms reduce visibility on highways.","explanation":"Agreement.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Sandstorms reduce visibility on highways.","Sandstorms reduces visibility on highways.","Sandstorms reducing visibility on highways.","Sandstorms is reduce visibility on highways."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Why do reports use headings?","answer":"to help readers find information quickly","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"لتسهيل إيجاد المعلومات.","options":["to make the report longer","to replace topic sentences","to show the writer's opinion","to help readers find information quickly"],"dok":2,"skill":"text features"},{"q":"Which sentence does NOT belong in the conclusion of a report?","answer":"A new study from 2024 found that floods are increasing.","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"معلومة جديدة.","options":["By preparing early, families can protect themselves.","Flash floods can be dangerous, but people can stay safe.","A new study from 2024 found that floods are increasing.","Understanding the danger is the first step."],"dok":2,"skill":"conclusions"},{"q":"Which sentence uses an objective tone suitable for a report?","answer":"Heat waves can cause dehydration.","explanation":"Tone.","explanation_ar":"أسلوب موضوعي.","options":["Heat waves are totally the worst, guys!","I personally hate heat waves.","Heat waves can cause dehydration.","Heat waves are scary, right?"],"dok":2,"skill":"formal tone"},{"q":"A student's report puts safety advice in the *What Is a Flash Flood?* section. What is the BEST advice?","answer":"Move the advice to the *How to Stay Safe* section.","explanation":"Revising.","explanation_ar":"انقل النصائح إلى القسم المناسب.","options":["Delete all the headings.","Add more advice to the introduction.","Change the title.","Move the advice to the *How to Stay Safe* section."],"dok":3,"skill":"organizing"},{"q":"How does a report with headings help communities prepare for disasters?","answer":"People can quickly find the information they need, such as safety advice, in an emergency.","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"يجد الناس المعلومة بسرعة.","options":["People can quickly find the information they need, such as safety advice, in an emergency.","It makes disasters less likely to happen.","It replaces official warnings.","It is only useful for teachers."],"dok":3,"skill":"theme"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'report': a neat document with a title and headings"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'heading': a page with bold titles above paragraphs"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'section': a cake cut into labeled pieces"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'topic sentence': the first sentence of a paragraph highlighted"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'flash flood': brown water rushing through a dry valley"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'precaution': a family packing a first-aid kit"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'third person': pronoun cards: he, she, it, they"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS4-01","prompt":"A flash flood filling a desert wadi."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS4-02","prompt":"The parts of a report with headings."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS4-03","prompt":"Sorting notes under headings before writing."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V01","script":"report. Noura wrote a report about flash floods."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V02","script":"heading. Each heading tells you what the section is about."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V03","script":"section. The report has five sections."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V04","script":"topic sentence. Start each section with a topic sentence."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V05","script":"flash flood. A flash flood filled the wadi in minutes."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V06","script":"precaution. An emergency kit is a useful precaution."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS4-V07","script":"third person. Reports are usually written in the third person."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS4-T1","script":"**Introduction** – Saudi Arabia is famous for its deserts, so many people are surprised to learn that floods are one of its most serious natural hazards. This report explains what flash floods are, why they are dangerous and how families can stay safe. **What Is a Flash Flood?** – A flash flood is a flood that develops very quickly, often within minutes or a few hours of heavy rain. In dry areas, the hard ground cannot absorb much water, so rain runs off quickly into wadis and streets. A dry wadi can become a fast river in a very short time. **Why Are Flash Floods Dangerous?** – Moving water is much more powerful than it looks. According to the US National Weather Service, just 15 centimeters of fast-moving water can knock an adult down, and about 30 centimeters can carry away a small car. Floodwater may also hide holes, broken roads and fallen power lines. **How to Stay Safe** – Families should follow warnings from Saudi Arabia's National Center for Meteorology and the civil defense. During heavy rain, people should stay away from wadis and low roads, never walk or drive through moving water and move to higher ground if water rises. A home emergency kit with water, a flashlight and a first-aid kit is a useful precaution. **Conclusion** – Flash floods are rare in many places, but they can be deadly when they happen. By understanding the danger and preparing early, families can protect themselves."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS4-VID","script":"Floods in the desert? Yes! And a good report can help families stay safe. Introduction. What Is a Flash Flood? Why Are Flash Floods Dangerous? How to Stay Safe. Conclusion. Each heading answers one question. Each section starts with a topic sentence. Facts with sources, third person, objective tone – no 'I think' and no 'you guys'. A clear report helps readers find what they need – fast!"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS5","title":"Writing Workshop: A four-paragraph emergency-preparedness report with headings","lesson_type":"writing_workshop","objectives":["plan an emergency-preparedness report from a fact file with the Report Planner","write a four-section report (200–250 words) with headings, topic sentences, cause/effect language and clear advice","revise for organization and objective tone, edit and share the report in a Family Safety Guide"],"objectives_ar":["التخطيط لتقرير عن الاستعداد للطوارئ من ملف معلومات بمخطط التقرير.","كتابة تقرير من أربعة أقسام (200–250 كلمة) بعناوين وجمل افتتاحية ولغة سبب ونتيجة ونصائح واضحة.","تحسين التقرير في التنظيم والموضوعية وتدقيقه ومشاركته في دليل سلامة الأسرة."],"success_criteria":["My report has four sections with clear, parallel headings.","Each section starts with a topic sentence; my body sections explain the danger (causes and effects) and the safety advice.","My tone is objective and third person; my conclusion adds no new facts; the report is 200–250 words."],"success_criteria_ar":["تقريري فيه أربعة أقسام بعناوين واضحة ومتوازية.","يبدأ كل قسم بجملة افتتاحية، ويشرح قسما العرض الخطر (الأسباب والنتائج) ونصائح السلامة.","أسلوبي موضوعي بضمير الغائب، وخاتمتي بلا معلومات جديدة، والتقرير في 200–250 كلمة."],"map_area":"Language Usage: Write, Revise Texts for Purpose and Audience (cross-strand)","vocabulary":[{"word":"hazard","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈhæzərd/","definition":"something that could be dangerous or cause harm","arabic":"خطر / مصدر خطر","example":"Floods and heat waves are natural hazards.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"heat wave","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈhit ˌweɪv/","definition":"a period of unusually hot weather that lasts several days","arabic":"موجة حر","example":"During the heat wave, people stayed indoors at midday.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"heatstroke","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈhitˌstroʊk/","definition":"a dangerous illness caused by the body becoming too hot","arabic":"ضربة الشمس / ضربة الحر","example":"Heatstroke is a medical emergency.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"visibility","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌvɪzəˈbɪləti/","definition":"how far or how clearly you can see","arabic":"مدى الرؤية","example":"Visibility dropped to a few meters in the sandstorm.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"smoke alarm","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsmoʊk əˌlɑrm/","definition":"a device that makes a loud sound when it detects smoke","arabic":"جهاز إنذار الدخان","example":"Test your smoke alarm every month.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"escape route","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ɪˈskeɪp ˌrut/","definition":"a planned way to leave a dangerous place","arabic":"مخرج / طريق النجاة","example":"Every room should have two escape routes.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"drill","pos":"noun","ipa":"/drɪl/","definition":"a practice of what to do in an emergency","arabic":"تمرين / تدريب (إخلاء)","example":"Our school holds a fire drill every term.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"An **emergency-preparedness report** gives families **clear, organized facts** about a hazard and **practical advice** for staying safe. People may read it quickly or in a stressful moment, so it must be **easy to scan**: a clear **title**, **parallel headings** and **short, direct sentences**.\n\n**Section 1 – Introduction:** a hook and a sentence that says what the report covers. **Section 2 – Why Is It Dangerous?:** what the hazard is, its **causes** and **effects**, and who is most **vulnerable** (*As a result, … / This means that…*). **Section 3 – How to Stay Safe:** precautions before, during and after (*should, must, never*). **Section 4 – Conclusion:** the main idea and a final message – **no new facts**.\n\nStart each section with a **topic sentence**. Use **cause/effect signal words** to explain the danger, **imperatives or *should*** for advice, the **third person** and an **objective tone**. Mention where facts come from (*according to…*, *health experts advise…*) and keep the report **200–250 words**.","examples":["Title: ***Staying Safe in a Heat Wave***","Introduction (purpose): ***This report explains why heat waves are a risk and how families can prepare for them.***","Topic sentence (danger): ***A heat wave is a period of unusually hot weather that lasts several days.***","Cause/effect: ***When the body gets too hot, it loses water and salt through sweat. As a result, people can suffer from heat exhaustion.***","Who is vulnerable: ***Young children, older people and outdoor workers are the most vulnerable.***","Topic sentence (advice): ***Families can take simple precautions.***","Advice with a reason: ***Children must never be left in a parked car, because the temperature inside can rise very quickly.***","Source: ***Families should follow heat warnings from the National Center for Meteorology.***","Conclusion: ***Most heat illness can be prevented.***"],"common_errors":["✗ No headings, or headings like *Part 2*. → ✓ Use clear, parallel headings. (Easy to scan.)","✗ Mixing danger facts and advice in one section. → ✓ Keep danger in Section 2 and advice in Section 3. (One section, one job.)","✗ Advice without reasons: *Don't go out.* → ✓ Avoid the sun at midday, because heatstroke is a risk. (Advice + reason.)","✗ You will totally die in a sandstorm! → ✓ Sandstorms can make driving very dangerous. (Objective, calm tone.)","✗ New facts in the conclusion. → ✓ Sum up and give a final message. (No new facts.)","✗ Smoke rise, so stay low. → ✓ Smoke rises, so stay low. (Singular subject: **rises**.)","✗ Writing 350 words. → ✓ Keep it 200–250 words. (Check the length.)"],"arabic":"تقرير الاستعداد للطوارئ يقدّم للأسر حقائق واضحة منظمة عن خطر ما ونصائح عملية للبقاء آمنين، وقد يُقرأ بسرعة أو في لحظة توتر، لذا يجب أن يكون سهل التصفح: عنوان واضح، وعناوين فرعية متوازية، وجمل قصيرة مباشرة.\n\nالقسم الأول: مقدمة وجملة تبيّن ما يتناوله التقرير. الثاني: لماذا هو خطر؟ (تعريف الخطر وأسبابه ونتائجه والفئات الأكثر عرضة). الثالث: كيف تبقى آمنًا؟ (الاحتياطات قبل الخطر وأثناءه وبعده). الرابع: الخاتمة (الفكرة الرئيسة ورسالة أخيرة دون معلومات جديدة).\n\nابدأ كل قسم بجملة افتتاحية، واستخدم كلمات السبب والنتيجة لشرح الخطر، وصيغ الأمر أو should للنصائح، وضمير الغائب والأسلوب الموضوعي، واذكر مصادر المعلومات، والتزم بطول 200–250 كلمة.\n\nانتبه: اجعل لكل قسم وظيفة واحدة."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"Heat waves, sandstorms, earthquakes, fires at home – is your family ready?","on_screen":"My Emergency-Preparedness Report 🛟","visual":"A family gathered around a table with an emergency kit and a notebook."},{"time":"00:30–01:20","narration":"Choose your fact file: A, B, C or D.","on_screen":"Hazard Fact Files A–D","visual":"Four files open: a sun, a dust cloud, a cracked road, a smoke alarm."},{"time":"01:20–02:40","narration":"In summer, temperatures in parts of Saudi Arabia can rise above 45°C…","on_screen":"(model report)","visual":"A family drinking water indoors; a worker resting in the shade; a heat warning on a phone."},{"time":"02:40–03:30","narration":"Introduction. Why is it dangerous? How to stay safe. Conclusion. Four headings – one clear message.","on_screen":"4 sections with headings","visual":"Four labeled folders slide into a binder titled FAMILY SAFETY GUIDE."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Add your report to our Family Safety Guide – and take a copy home!","on_screen":"Family Safety Guide 📘","visual":"Students binding reports into a class guide."}],"captions_ar":["موجات الحر، والعواصف الرملية، والزلازل، وحرائق المنازل: هل أسرتك مستعدة؟","اختر ملف المعلومات: أ أو ب أو ج أو د.","في الصيف قد تتجاوز درجات الحرارة 45 درجة مئوية في بعض مناطق المملكة…","المقدمة، لماذا هو خطر؟ كيف تبقى آمنًا؟ الخاتمة: أربعة عناوين ورسالة واحدة واضحة.","أضف تقريرك إلى دليل سلامة الأسرة الصفي، وخذ نسخة إلى البيت!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, calm, reassuring female voice; a student reads the model report; speed 1.0."},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Hazard Fact Files","body":"**File A – Heat Waves** · In summer, temperatures in parts of Saudi Arabia can rise above 45°C. · A heat wave is a period of unusually hot weather that lasts several days. · Heat exhaustion causes heavy sweating, dizziness and headaches; heatstroke is a medical emergency. · Young children, older people and outdoor workers are the most vulnerable. · Advice: drink water regularly, avoid the sun in the hottest hours, wear light, loose clothing, never leave children in parked cars and follow warnings from the National Center for Meteorology.\n\n**File B – Sandstorms** · Strong winds can lift huge amounts of sand and dust, especially in spring. · Visibility on roads can drop to a few meters. · Dust can make breathing difficult, especially for people with asthma. · Advice: stay indoors and close windows, wear a mask outside, drive slowly or stop in a safe place, and follow official weather warnings.\n\n**File C – Earthquakes** · Use the facts from *When the Earth Shakes* (Lesson 2). · Advice: during shaking, drop, cover and hold on; stay away from windows; after the shaking stops, move to an open area away from buildings; keep an emergency kit with water, food, a flashlight and a first-aid kit.\n\n**File D – Fires at Home** · Smoke rises, so the cleanest air is near the floor. · Smoke alarms give people early warning, especially at night. · Advice: test smoke alarms regularly, plan two ways out of every room, crawl low under smoke, never use an elevator during a fire, and if clothes catch fire, stop, drop and roll. Call the civil defense as soon as everyone is safe.","word_count":259,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS5-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L07-U4-LS5-1","genre":"fact files","role":"model","difficulty":"800L–950L","glossary":[],"annotations":["Choose **one file**. You will write an **emergency-preparedness report** for families.","Use **four sections with headings**: (1) **Introduction** – hook + what the report covers; (2) **Why Is It Dangerous?** – causes and effects; (3) **How to Stay Safe** – precautions; (4) **Conclusion** – main idea and a final message.","Begin each section with a **topic sentence**, use **cause/effect signal words**, keep an **objective, third-person tone** and mention where your facts come from."]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS5-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the fact files and the model. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ ملفات المعلومات والنموذج واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"According to File A, how high can summer temperatures rise in parts of Saudi Arabia?","answer":"above 45°C","explanation":"File A.","explanation_ar":"أكثر من 45 درجة.","options":["above 15°C","above 45°C","above 100°C","above 25°C"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"According to File B, in which season are sandstorms especially common?","answer":"spring","explanation":"File B.","explanation_ar":"الربيع.","options":["winter","spring","autumn","never"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Why should people crawl low under smoke, according to File D?","answer":"Smoke rises, so the cleanest air is near the floor.","explanation":"File D.","explanation_ar":"لأن الدخان يرتفع.","options":["Crawling is faster than walking.","The floor is cooler.","Smoke rises, so the cleanest air is near the floor.","Smoke alarms are on the floor."],"dok":1,"skill":"cause and effect","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What should you do if your clothes catch fire, according to File D?","answer":"stop, drop and roll","explanation":"File D.","explanation_ar":"قف وانبطح وتدحرج.","options":["stop, drop and roll","run as fast as you can","use the elevator","open all the windows"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What should people do right after the shaking of an earthquake stops, according to File C?","answer":"move to an open area away from buildings","explanation":"File C.","explanation_ar":"الانتقال إلى مكان مفتوح.","options":["go back inside to sleep","stand next to the windows","move to an open area away from buildings","use the elevator to go downstairs"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"In the model report, which heading would help a reader find the signs of heat exhaustion?","answer":"Why Are Heat Waves Dangerous?","explanation":"Headings.","explanation_ar":"لماذا موجات الحر خطرة؟","options":["How to Stay Safe","Conclusion","Introduction","Why Are Heat Waves Dangerous?"],"dok":2,"skill":"text features","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS5-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Plan your report. Choose.","instructions_ar":"خطط لتقريرك: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which is the BEST title for a report based on File B?","answer":"Staying Safe in a Sandstorm","explanation":"Title.","explanation_ar":"أفضل عنوان.","options":["Sand","My Report","Wow, Dust!","Staying Safe in a Sandstorm"],"dok":2,"skill":"headings","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which is the BEST topic sentence for *Why Are Sandstorms Dangerous?*","answer":"Sandstorms are dangerous because they reduce visibility and make breathing difficult.","explanation":"Topic sentence.","explanation_ar":"أفضل جملة افتتاحية.","options":["Sandstorms are dangerous because they reduce visibility and make breathing difficult.","I saw a sandstorm last year.","Sand is yellow.","Stay indoors and close the windows."],"dok":2,"skill":"topic sentences","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence gives advice WITH a reason?","answer":"Drivers should slow down in a sandstorm, because visibility can drop to a few meters.","explanation":"Advice + reason.","explanation_ar":"نصيحة مع سببها.","options":["Drivers should slow down.","Sandstorms happen in spring.","Visibility is important.","Drivers should slow down in a sandstorm, because visibility can drop to a few meters."],"dok":2,"skill":"explaining advice","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence uses cause/effect language about fires correctly?","answer":"Because smoke rises, the cleanest air is near the floor.","explanation":"Cause/effect.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Because smoke rises the cleanest air is near the floor because.","Because smoke rises, the cleanest air is near the floor.","Smoke rises, as a result of the floor.","The floor causes the smoke."],"dok":2,"skill":"cause and effect","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about smoke alarms is grammatically correct?","answer":"A smoke alarm warns people early, especially at night.","explanation":"Agreement.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["A smoke alarm warns people early, especially at night.","A smoke alarm warn people early, especially at night.","A smoke alarm warning people early, especially at night.","A smoke alarm are warning people early, especially at night."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar","stage":"guided"},{"q":"A partner's conclusion adds *Also, dust storms can damage car engines.* What is the BEST advice?","answer":"Move this new fact to a body section; the conclusion should only sum up.","explanation":"Revising.","explanation_ar":"انقل المعلومة الجديدة.","options":["Add more new facts to the conclusion.","Delete the headings.","Move this new fact to a body section; the conclusion should only sum up.","Change the title to match it."],"dok":3,"skill":"revising","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS5-A3","type":"organizer","stage":"freer","instructions":"Organizer: Plan with the Report Planner.","instructions_ar":"خطط بمخطط التقرير.","items":[{"q":"File B: write the title and the introduction (hook + purpose).","answer":"Title: Staying Safe in a Sandstorm. Introduction: In spring, a wall of dust can turn a sunny afternoon orange in minutes. This report explains why sandstorms are dangerous and how families can stay safe.","explanation":"Introduction.","explanation_ar":"المقدمة.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"File B: write notes for *Why Are Sandstorms Dangerous?* with two cause/effect links.","answer":"strong winds lift sand and dust → visibility drops to a few meters → driving becomes dangerous; dust in the air → breathing becomes difficult, especially for people with asthma","explanation":"Danger.","explanation_ar":"الخطر.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"File B: write notes for *How to Stay Safe* with reasons.","answer":"stay indoors and close windows (keeps dust out); wear a mask outside (protects breathing); drive slowly or stop safely (visibility is low); follow official warnings (to prepare early)","explanation":"Advice.","explanation_ar":"النصائح.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"File B: write a conclusion with a final message.","answer":"Sandstorms cannot be stopped, but their dangers can be reduced. By preparing early and following warnings, families can stay safe when the dust arrives.","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"الخاتمة.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS5-A4","type":"writing","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write, revise, edit and share your report.","instructions_ar":"اكتب تقريرك وحسّنه ودققه وشاركه.","items":[{"q":"Write the Introduction section with its heading.","answer":"Introduction – In spring, a wall of dust can turn a sunny afternoon orange in minutes. Sandstorms are common in many parts of Saudi Arabia. This report explains why they are dangerous and how families can stay safe.","explanation":"Section 1.","explanation_ar":"القسم الأول.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write the *Why Is It Dangerous?* section with its heading.","answer":"Why Are Sandstorms Dangerous? – Sandstorms are dangerous because they reduce visibility and make breathing difficult. When strong winds lift sand and dust, drivers may see only a few meters ahead. As a result, accidents become more likely. The dust can also irritate the lungs, especially for people with asthma.","explanation":"Section 2.","explanation_ar":"القسم الثاني.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write the *How to Stay Safe* and Conclusion sections with headings.","answer":"How to Stay Safe – Families can take several precautions. They should stay indoors and close windows, because this keeps dust out. People who must go outside should wear a mask. Drivers should slow down or stop in a safe place. Conclusion – Sandstorms cannot be stopped, but by preparing early and following warnings, families can stay safe.","explanation":"Sections 3–4.","explanation_ar":"القسمان الثالث والرابع.","dok":3,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write one sentence you changed to make your tone more objective (before → after).","answer":"Before: You guys will choke in a sandstorm! After: Dust in the air can make breathing difficult, especially for people with asthma.","explanation":"Revision.","explanation_ar":"التحسين.","dok":3,"skill":"revising","stage":"production","_w":{"checklist":[],"min":null,"max":null}}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS5-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Report Planner","instructions":"Organizer: Choose one fact file. Plan each section with a heading and a topic sentence, then add notes.","items":[{"q":"Title","answer":"Staying Safe in a Heat Wave"},{"q":"Section 1 – Introduction (hook + purpose)","answer":"temperatures above 45°C; homes are cool, but extreme heat is still dangerous; this report explains the risk and how to prepare"},{"q":"Section 2 – Why Is It Dangerous? (causes, effects, who is vulnerable)","answer":"heat wave = several unusually hot days; sweat → loss of water and salt → heat exhaustion; heatstroke = emergency; children, older people, outdoor workers"},{"q":"Section 3 – How to Stay Safe (advice + reasons)","answer":"drink water regularly; avoid the hottest hours; light, loose clothes and a hat; never leave children in parked cars; follow NCM warnings"},{"q":"Section 4 – Conclusion (main idea + final message)","answer":"heat waves are serious, but most heat illness can be prevented; every family can stay safe"},{"q":"Signal words and sources I will use","answer":"when, as a result, because, should, must never; the National Center for Meteorology"}]},{"id":"RW-L07-U4-LS5-WR","type":"writing","instructions":"Writing: Choose one Hazard Fact File. Write an emergency-preparedness report for families (200–250 words) with a title and four sections with headings: (1) Introduction; (2) Why Is It Dangerous?; (3) How to Stay Safe; (4) Conclusion. Begin each section with a topic sentence, explain causes and effects, give advice with reasons and keep an objective, third-person tone. Choose one Hazard Fact File. Write an emergency-preparedness report for families (200–250 words) with a title and four sections with headings: (1) Introduction; (2) Why Is It Dangerous?; (3) How to Stay Safe; (4) Conclusion. Begin each section with a topic sentence, explain causes and effects, give advice with reasons and keep an objective, third-person tone.","instructions_ar":"اختر ملفًا من ملفات معلومات المخاطر، واكتب تقريرًا للأسر عن الاستعداد للطوارئ (200–250 كلمة) بعنوان رئيس وأربعة أقسام بعناوين فرعية: (1) مقدمة، (2) لماذا هو خطر؟، (3) كيف تبقى آمنًا؟، (4) خاتمة. ابدأ كل قسم بجملة افتتاحية، واشرح الأسباب والنتائج، وقدّم نصائح مع أسبابها، وحافظ على أسلوب موضوعي بضمير الغائب.","prompt":"Choose one Hazard Fact File. Write an emergency-preparedness report for families (200–250 words) with a title and four sections with headings: (1) Introduction; (2) Why Is It Dangerous?; (3) How to Stay Safe; (4) Conclusion. Begin each section with a topic sentence, explain causes and effects, give advice with reasons and keep an objective, third-person tone.","checklist":["My report has a clear title and four sections with parallel headings.","My introduction has a hook and says what the report covers.","Each section begins with a topic sentence.","I explained causes and effects with signal words (when, as a result, because).","My safety advice includes reasons.","I used an objective, third-person tone and named at least one source.","My conclusion sums up the main idea and adds no new facts.","My report is 200–250 words, and I checked spelling, punctuation and subject–verb agreement."],"rubric_id":"RW-RUBRIC-5T","min_words":200,"max_words":275,"organizer":"Report Planner (title – introduction – why dangerous – how to stay safe – conclusion)","items":[{"q":"Choose one Hazard Fact File. Write an emergency-preparedness report for families (200–250 words) with a title and four sections with headings: (1) Introduction; (2) Why Is It Dangerous?; (3) How to Stay Safe; (4) Conclusion. Begin each section with a topic sentence, explain causes and effects, give advice with reasons and keep an objective, third-person tone.","answer":"**Introduction** – In summer, temperatures in parts of Saudi Arabia can rise above 45°C. Most people stay comfortable in air-conditioned homes, but extreme heat can still be dangerous. This report explains why heat waves are a risk and how families can prepare for them. (new paragraph) **Why Are Heat Waves Dangerous?** – A heat wave is a period of unusually hot weather that lasts several days. When the body gets too hot, it loses water and salt through sweat. As a result, people can suffer from heat exhaustion, which causes heavy sweating, dizziness and headaches. If the body cannot cool down, heatstroke can follow, and this is a medical emergency. Young children, older people and workers who spend long hours outdoors are the most vulnerable. (new paragraph) **How to Stay Safe** – Families can take simple precautions. They should drink water regularly, even before they feel thirsty, and avoid the sun during the hottest hours of the day. Light, loose clothing and a hat help the body stay cool. Children must never be left in a parked car, because the temperature inside can rise very quickly. Families should also follow heat warnings from the National Center for Meteorology. (new paragraph) **Conclusion** – Heat waves are a serious natural hazard, but most heat illness can be prevented. By drinking enough water, avoiding the midday sun and watching out for vulnerable people, every family can stay safe this summer.","_w":{"checklist":["My report has a clear title and four sections with parallel headings.","My introduction has a hook and says what the report covers.","Each section begins with a topic sentence.","I explained causes and effects with signal words (when, as a result, because).","My safety advice includes reasons.","I used an objective, third-person tone and named at least one source.","My conclusion sums up the main idea and adds no new facts.","My report is 200–250 words, and I checked spelling, punctuation and subject–verb agreement."],"min":200,"max":275}}]}],"game":{"name":"Safety Guide Challenge","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Scan race: a question appears and the child must tap the correct heading and then the sentence that answers it in a model report; fast, accurate scanning earns badges for the Family Safety Guide.","Reports are placed in the class Family Safety Guide.","A team reads a scan-race question card.","Another team has 20 seconds to find the answer in one of the reports by using the headings.","If the answer is found in time, both the finding team and the report's writer earn a point.","A report whose headings helped readers find three answers earns a 'Clear Writer' badge."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is a *hazard*?","answer":"something that could be dangerous or cause harm","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"خطر.","options":["a kind of report","something that could be dangerous or cause harm","a safe place","a weather forecast"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"What is an *escape route*?","answer":"a planned way to leave a dangerous place","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"طريق النجاة.","options":["a road for racing","a list of hazards","a type of alarm","a planned way to leave a dangerous place"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*Visibility dropped to a few meters in the sandstorm.* *Visibility* means ___.","answer":"how far or how clearly you can see","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"مدى الرؤية.","options":["how hot the air is","how loud the wind is","how far or how clearly you can see","how fast the car moves"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which section of an emergency report should give the safety advice?","answer":"How to Stay Safe","explanation":"Structure.","explanation_ar":"كيف تبقى آمنًا.","options":["Introduction","How to Stay Safe","Conclusion","the title"],"dok":1,"skill":"report structure"},{"q":"Which sentence about heatstroke is correct?","answer":"Heatstroke is a medical emergency.","explanation":"Grammar.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Heatstroke are a medical emergency.","Heatstroke is a medical emergency.","Heatstroke being a medical emergency.","Heatstroke is a medical emergencies."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which sentence explains a CAUSE and its EFFECT?","answer":"When the body gets too hot, it loses water through sweat.","explanation":"Cause/effect.","explanation_ar":"سبب ونتيجة.","options":["Heat waves happen in summer.","Drink water.","When the body gets too hot, it loses water through sweat.","This report is about heat."],"dok":2,"skill":"cause and effect"},{"q":"Why should safety advice include reasons?","answer":"Reasons help people understand the danger and follow the advice.","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"تساعد على الفهم والالتزام.","options":["Reasons make the report longer.","Reasons replace the headings.","Reasons are only for scientists.","Reasons help people understand the danger and follow the advice."],"dok":2,"skill":"explaining advice"},{"q":"Which heading is parallel with *What Is a Heat Wave?* and *Why Are Heat Waves Dangerous?*","answer":"How Can Families Stay Safe?","explanation":"Parallel headings.","explanation_ar":"عنوان متوازٍ.","options":["Safety Stuff","Part Three!","How Can Families Stay Safe?","I Like Cold Weather"],"dok":2,"skill":"headings"},{"q":"A report says *You will definitely get sick if you go outside!* What is the BEST advice?","answer":"Use a calm, objective tone and accurate information.","explanation":"Revising.","explanation_ar":"أسلوب هادئ موضوعي.","options":["Use a calm, objective tone and accurate information.","Add more exclamation marks.","Write it in capital letters.","Remove all advice."],"dok":3,"skill":"formal tone"},{"q":"How do clear reports help communities prepare for and recover from disasters?","answer":"They give people accurate information and practical steps they can follow quickly.","explanation":"Theme connection.","explanation_ar":"معلومات دقيقة وخطوات عملية.","options":["They give people accurate information and practical steps they can follow quickly.","They stop disasters from happening.","They replace the civil defense.","They are only useful after a disaster."],"dok":3,"skill":"theme"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'hazard': a yellow triangle warning sign"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'heat wave': a thermometer showing a very high temperature under a blazing sun"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'heatstroke': a person resting in the shade with water and a cold towel"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'visibility': a road disappearing into orange dust"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'smoke alarm': a white smoke alarm on a ceiling"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'escape route': a floor plan with green arrows to the exits"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'drill': students walking calmly out of a school"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS5-01","prompt":"A class Family Safety Guide."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS5-02","prompt":"Four hazard fact files."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L07-U4-LS5-03","prompt":"Heat-wave safety precautions."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V01","script":"hazard. Floods and heat waves are natural hazards."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V02","script":"heat wave. During the heat wave, people stayed indoors at midday."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V03","script":"heatstroke. Heatstroke is a medical emergency."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V04","script":"visibility. Visibility dropped to a few meters in the sandstorm."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V05","script":"smoke alarm. Test your smoke alarm every month."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V06","script":"escape route. Every room should have two escape routes."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS5-V07","script":"drill. Our school holds a fire drill every term."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS5-T1","script":"**File A – Heat Waves** · In summer, temperatures in parts of Saudi Arabia can rise above 45°C. · A heat wave is a period of unusually hot weather that lasts several days. · Heat exhaustion causes heavy sweating, dizziness and headaches; heatstroke is a medical emergency. · Young children, older people and outdoor workers are the most vulnerable. · Advice: drink water regularly, avoid the sun in the hottest hours, wear light, loose clothing, never leave children in parked cars and follow warnings from the National Center for Meteorology. **File B – Sandstorms** · Strong winds can lift huge amounts of sand and dust, especially in spring. · Visibility on roads can drop to a few meters. · Dust can make breathing difficult, especially for people with asthma. · Advice: stay indoors and close windows, wear a mask outside, drive slowly or stop in a safe place, and follow official weather warnings. **File C – Earthquakes** · Use the facts from *When the Earth Shakes* (Lesson 2). · Advice: during shaking, drop, cover and hold on; stay away from windows; after the shaking stops, move to an open area away from buildings; keep an emergency kit with water, food, a flashlight and a first-aid kit. **File D – Fires at Home** · Smoke rises, so the cleanest air is near the floor. · Smoke alarms give people early warning, especially at night. · Advice: test smoke alarms regularly, plan two ways out of every room, crawl low under smoke, never use an elevator during a fire, and if clothes catch fire, stop, drop and roll. Call the civil defense as soon as everyone is safe."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L07-U4-LS5-VID","script":"Heat waves, sandstorms, earthquakes, fires at home – is your family ready? Choose your fact file: A, B, C or D. In summer, temperatures in parts of Saudi Arabia can rise above 45°C… Introduction. Why is it dangerous? How to stay safe. Conclusion. Four headings – one clear message. Add your report to our Family Safety Guide – and take a copy home!"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"Workers spent two days trying to ___ water and electricity to the flooded neighborhood.","answer":"restore","explanation":"Restore.","explanation_ar":"إعادة.","options":["restore","predict","evacuate","forecast"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"The ___ for tomorrow says there will be strong winds and dust in the afternoon.","answer":"forecast","explanation":"Forecast.","explanation_ar":"النشرة الجوية.","options":["survivor","landslide","forecast","shelter"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"After the storm, trucks of ___ arrived with water, food and blankets.","answer":"relief","explanation":"Relief.","explanation_ar":"إغاثة.","options":["magnitude","hazard","relief","drill"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Our school holds a fire ___ every term so that everyone knows how to leave the building safely.","answer":"drill","explanation":"Drill.","explanation_ar":"تمرين.","options":["drill","fault","crust","forecast"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Heavy rain on the mountain caused a ___, and rocks blocked the road.","answer":"landslide","explanation":"Landslide.","explanation_ar":"انهيار أرضي.","options":["heat wave","landslide","smoke alarm","tsunami"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*A **hydrologist** studied the wadi after the storm.* Which word parts help you understand *hydrologist*?","answer":"hydro (water) + ologist (expert)","explanation":"Word parts.","explanation_ar":"ماء + مختص.","options":["hydro (fire) + ologist (study)","hydro (water) + ologist (expert)","hy (high) + drologist (doctor)","hydrol (storm) + ogist (machine)"],"dok":2,"skill":"word parts","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*The civil defense **coordinated** the work of the rescue teams.* *Coordinated* means ___.","answer":"organized so that they worked well together","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"نسّق.","options":["stopped completely","argued with","forgot about","organized so that they worked well together"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary in context","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Which instrument records the shaking of the ground?","answer":"a seismograph","explanation":"Word parts.","explanation_ar":"مِرسمة الزلازل.","options":["a thermometer","a compass","a smoke alarm","a seismograph"],"dok":2,"skill":"word parts","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Which is the BEST heading for a report section about what to pack before a storm?","answer":"What to Pack Before a Storm","explanation":"Headings.","explanation_ar":"أفضل عنوان.","options":["Stuff","Section","What to Pack Before a Storm","Storms Are Scary!"],"dok":1,"skill":"headings","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which is the BEST topic sentence for a section called *Why Are Cyclones Dangerous?*","answer":"Cyclones are dangerous because they bring destructive winds, heavy rain and flooding.","explanation":"Topic sentence.","explanation_ar":"أفضل جملة افتتاحية.","options":["I saw a cyclone on TV.","Cyclones form over the ocean, you know.","Pack water and food.","Cyclones are dangerous because they bring destructive winds, heavy rain and flooding."],"dok":1,"skill":"topic sentences","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which sentence uses a cause/effect signal word correctly?","answer":"The ground was dry and hard; as a result, the rain rushed into the wadi.","explanation":"Signal words.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The ground was dry and hard; as a result, the rain rushed into the wadi.","The ground was dry and hard; as a result the, rain rushed into the wadi.","As a result, the ground was dry because the rain.","The rain rushed; as a result of."],"dok":2,"skill":"cause and effect","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which sentence about coastal storms keeps an objective, third-person tone?","answer":"Residents should leave low coastal areas when officials give a warning.","explanation":"Tone.","explanation_ar":"أسلوب موضوعي.","options":["You guys better run when the storm comes!","I think cyclones are terrifying.","Residents should leave low coastal areas when officials give a warning.","Honestly, storms are the worst."],"dok":2,"skill":"formal tone","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which sentence is grammatically correct?","answer":"Satellites watch storms from space.","explanation":"Agreement.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Satellites watches storms from space.","Satellites watch storms from space.","Satellites watching storms from space.","Satellites is watch storms from space."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"A student's report has the headings *Introduction / Danger! / you must be safe / The End*. What is the BEST advice?","answer":"Make the headings clear and parallel, for example *Introduction / Why Are Storms Dangerous? / How to Stay Safe / Conclusion*.","explanation":"Revising.","explanation_ar":"اجعل العناوين واضحة ومتوازية.","options":["Make the headings clear and parallel, for example *Introduction / Why Are Storms Dangerous? / How to Stay Safe / Conclusion*.","Remove all the headings.","Add more exclamation marks.","Write the headings in Arabic only."],"dok":3,"skill":"headings","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"}],"unit_writing":{"task":"Use information from *Hurricanes, Typhoons and Cyclones* (and *When the Warning Came*) to write an emergency-preparedness report for families living on a coast (200–250 words). Give it a title and four sections with headings: (1) Introduction; (2) Why Are Tropical Cyclones Dangerous?; (3) How to Prepare and Stay Safe; (4) Conclusion. Begin each section with a topic sentence, use cause/effect signal words, give advice with reasons and keep an objective, third-person tone.","model":"**Introduction** – Tropical cyclones are among the most powerful storms on Earth, and they can also form in the Arabian Sea. Families who live near the coast need to understand them. This report explains why these storms are dangerous and how families can prepare for them. (new paragraph) **Why Are Tropical Cyclones Dangerous?** – Tropical cyclones are dangerous because they bring destructive winds, heavy rain and flooding. When a strong storm reaches land, its winds can blow at more than 119 kilometers per hour. In addition, a storm surge can push ocean water onto the coast, so low areas may flood very quickly. (new paragraph) **How to Prepare and Stay Safe** – Families can take several important steps. First, they should follow official weather warnings, because forecasters can often predict a storm's path several days ahead. Before the storm, they should secure loose objects outside and gather water, food, a flashlight and a first-aid kit. If officials advise people to leave low coastal areas, families must evacuate immediately. Finally, they should check on elderly neighbors who may need help. (new paragraph) **Conclusion** – Tropical cyclones cannot be stopped, but their effects can be reduced. As Salem learned in *When the Warning Came*, the most important rule is simple: when the warning comes, believe it.","marks":20}});
